\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-FACTBOX-What is Argentina's peso really worth?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alejandro Lifschitz   \par \uc2 1,133 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2011\par 12:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ( Repeats Tuesday story for more subscribers )\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Nov 22 (Reuters) - Argentina's peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades in several markets, and for most of the last eight years, the exchange rates in these markets have been much the same.\par \par \uc2 However, the spread, or difference, between the peso's value in these markets has widened sharply since Oct. 31, when President Cristina Fernandez slapped tough new controls on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases after months of brisk capital flight. [ID:nN1E79U0X9] [ID:nN1E7A80OQ]\par \par \uc2 Between August and October, when Fernandez won easy re-election, capital flight surged to an estimated $10 billion as investors bet she would allow the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depreciate at a faster rate after the election.\par \par \uc2 Inflation estimated privately at about 25 percent is fast eroding the competitiveness of Argentine exports and has easily outstripped the peso's nominal rate of depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Capital flight prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shell out some $4.8 billion in foreign reserves in recent months to prop up the peso. Government officials fear a sudden depreciation could fuel inflation and exacerbate outflows.\par \par \uc2 Here are brief profiles of the different peso markets:\par \par \uc2 INTERBANK\par \par \uc2 This is the formal, spot market administered by the Open Electronic Market, or MAE by its Spanish initials. The main players are banks handling orders by large companies, including \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's grain exporters.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this market on a near-daily basis, more recently by selling dollars to keep the peso stable despite surging demand for greenbacks amid capital flight and fast-growing imports. In the last few days, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has resumed dollar purchases.\par \par \uc2 Sources at private banks said recently the government had asked big companies to keep out of the market for a few weeks to help ease pressure on the peso.\par \par \uc2 Trade volume has fallen to about $180 million per day from about $450 million before the controls on dollar purchases came into force. The formal peso <ARS=RASL> closed at a bid price of 4.2575 to the dollar, a 6.7 percent loss over the last year.\par \par \uc2 RETAIL\par \par \uc2 Operated by banks and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses, this market is generally used by individual savers and small- and medium-sized companies.\par \par \uc2 Under the new controls imposed three weeks ago, tax inspectors must pre-approve all \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the retail market. If the potential buyer has a record of unpaid taxes or is judged to have insufficient reported income, the transaction is rejected.\par \par \uc2 Government officials say the measure is aimed at combating rampant tax evasion and money laundering, but traders say it has acted as a barrier on dollar purchases. Trade volume has shrunk by about 80 percent from its pre-control levels of some $60 million per day.\par \par \uc2 In retail trade <ARSCC=>, the peso closed at 4.2400 on Tuesday, having shed 6.9 percent in the last 12 months.\par \par \uc2 PARALLEL MARKET\par \par \uc2 Under-the-counter deals are largely carried out by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign- exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses, where savers and small companies go to stay off the tax agency's radar since they are generally changing money that has not been declared to authorities.\par \par \uc2 In the first few days after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls were implemented, the informal or parallel peso, as measured by Reuters, slumped to a record low of 5 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 But government efforts since to tighten controls on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses effectively froze the market, which tended to move about $200 million a day.\par \par \uc2 Market sources have said the government is pressuring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders to sell the peso at around 4.40/4.50 per dollar in the informal market, well below the roughly 4.70 per dollar reported to be sought by holders of greenbacks.\par \par \uc2 No deals of any significance have been made on the informal market since Monday, Nov. 14, when the Argentine peso fetched 4.77/4.79 per dollar <ARSB=>. That represented a 7 percent decline since the controls were put into place.\par \par \uc2 BLUE-CHIP SWAP RATE\par \par \uc2 This is the implicit exchange rate involved in buying peso-denominated assets that trade in Argentina, mainly government bonds, and selling their counterparts on the market in New York to obtain dollars abroad.\par \par \uc2 This \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which some banks call the blue-chip swap rate, tends to reflect capital flight since investors use it to send money abroad without triggering capital controls.\par \par \uc2 As demand to get cash out of the country grows, the exchange rate for doing so tends to rise.\par \par \uc2 After the government controls were imposed, this implicit rate reached a record 5.19 pesos per dollar. It later eased back to its current level of around 4.70.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the rate cooled, thanks to state pensions agency ANSES, which heavily sold dollar-denominated bonds to reduce the gap between the price for these assets in pesos and their counterparts in dollars.\par \par \uc2 FUTURES MARKETS\par \par \uc2 There are two big futures markets in Argentina, one run by the MAE in which banks are the main actors, and the other one by Rofex, which is part of the Rosario exchange, and whose players include financial entities and agricultural exporters.\par \par \uc2 These markets move around $500 million a day, about 65 percent of which is traded on the Rofex.\par \par \uc2 As a way to avoid selling too many foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in the interbank market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  often makes deals on the futures market as part of its bid to tame the peso's volatility.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's close on the Rofex <0#ARS:> showed pesos for Nov. 30 settlement <ARSX11> at 4.2640 to the dollar and the peso for Sept. 28, 2012 settlement <ARSU12> at 4.8050 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 NON-DELIVERABLE FORWARD (NDF)\par \par \uc2 These over-the-counter futures let investors bet on changes in the value of the Argentine peso and are worth about $150 million a day, with most deals concentrated in near-term contracts.\par \par \uc2 NDF's are settled in dollars, meaning no pesos actually change hands. They are primarily used to hedge, or insure, against peso-dollar exchange-rate risk.\par \par \uc2 The implied yields on peso futures in New York <ARSNDFOR=> are much higher than what is seen in the Argentine futures markets, indicating there is a greater expectation that a steeper depreciation is in store.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the peso's NDFs traded at 4.3778 per dollar one month out, while six-month contracts fetched 4.9500 per dollar and 12-month deals were being made at 5.5800.\par \par \uc2 The implied yield, or return, on the 1-month peso NDF <ARS1MID=> is 33 percent a year, compared with less than 7 percent a year ago.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Jorge Otaola and Jeb Blount; Writing by Helen Popper and Hilary Burke; Editing by Jeb Blount and Jan Paschal)\par \par \uc2 ARGENTINA-PESO/ (FACTBOX) (REPEAT)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111126e7bn0009n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
