\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch: Argentina Policy Options Narrow With  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Restrictions\b0\par\par\uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2011\par 18:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Fitch Ratings said Wednesday that Argentina's implementation of stricter \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls might reduce the government's policy options as it tries to limit capital flight and defend the peso.\par \par \uc2 "Fitch Ratings sees the potential for a continuation of unorthodox economic policies as a growing risk for Argentina as it seeks to avoid a disorderly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation and rising inflation," the ratings firm said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The government on Monday tightened controls on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by requiring individuals and businesses to receive prior approval from the tax authority before buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The new control is the latest in a series of measures announced in recent days by the administration of President Cristina Kirchner to stem capital flight that has become a slow but steady drain on Argentina's international reserves.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the government ordered foreigners buying Argentine companies and real estate to deposit the full amount of the transaction in Argentina. Oil and mining companies now must also repatriate the proceeds of export sales, while insurance companies have to bring all offshore investment home before the year's end.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to gradually weaken the peso against the U.S. dollar to help exporters and limit inflation that is widely believed to be running above 20%.\par \par \uc2 The exchange rate is a politically sensitive issue in a country whose citizens still view the dollar as a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to Argentina's long history of violent devaluations and financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 After winning re-election last month with 54% of the vote, Kirchner faces a challenging second term amid a sluggish global economy and high inflation at home.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's economy is expected to grow at least 8% this year, though economic activity will slow next year, with the government forecasting a 5.1% expansion.\par \par \uc2 Fitch expects Argentina to report its first current account deficit this year since 2001 due in part to a shrinking trade surplus.\par \par \uc2 With the outlook for agricultural commodity prices and global growth weakening, Fitch said Argentina faces a more difficult export situation that could exacerbate external imbalances and declines in dollar reserves over coming months.\par \par \uc2 Fitch said it continues to view any shift to a more pragmatic policies as generally supportive of Argentina's ratings.\par \par \uc2 "However, ratings remain heavily constrained and could become vulnerable if a disorderly exchange rate adjustment or a significant ramp up in inflationary expectations destabilizes the macroeconomic environment," the ratings firm said.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740; ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 02-11-11 1711GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111102e7b2001aj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
