\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Stocks, Bonds Climb As Rebound Extends To Third Day\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shane Romig   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2011\par 00:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentine stocks and bonds notched up the third straight day of solid gains Thursday as the local market continues to claw back some of the heavy losses suffered in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's benchmark Merval stock index rose 2.3% to close at 2,596.64 points. Volume was moderate at 57 million pesos ($14 million.)\par \par \uc2 The fact that local equities gained despite the weakness overseas is a good sign that "genuine investors are returning and displacing the overseas traders that caused so much damage by aggressively liquidating their holdings during the latest global sell-off," economic consultancy Estudio Ber said in a market note.\par \par \uc2 Power company Edenor (EDN, EDN.BA) and energy holding group Transaner (TRAN.BA) posted some of the biggest gains. Edenor shot up 6.5% to close at ARS1.47, while Transaner added 5.4% to close at ARS1.18.\par \par \uc2 Bonds also closed mostly higher as investors snapped up the high-yielding government debt.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Thursday \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said the time is right to shift bond holdings from Venezuela to Argentina. Risk is up in Venezuela ahead of next year's election and Argentine bond prices are tempting after being beaten down, the firm said. Argentina "has been particularly -- and unfairly, in our view --penalized during the recent sell-off."\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2 ' pick is the short-maturity Boden 15, which "still trades at very depressed prices ... despite the rally in recent days, and offers an attractive, low price volatility combined with relatively high carry."\par \par \uc2 The Boden 15 ended the day 0.7% higher in price terms at ARS402, with the yield at 8.7%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the peso eased to ARS4.2135 to the U.S. dollar on Argentina's MAE \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  wholesale market, compared to ARS4.211. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to stem the peso's slide.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to pump foreign reserves into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem a decline in the peso, but a significant drop is still expected next year, RBS said in a market note.\par \par \uc2 Heavy capital flight ahead of October presidential elections has drained reserves and put the peso under pressure. The government's commitment to an orderly depreciation strategy will cause reserves to fall $7 billion this year and $8 billion next, with the peso ending 2012 at ARS5.11 to the dollar, RBS says.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stand at $48.2 billion, down more than $1.8 billion from Sept 1.\par \par \uc2 -By Shane Romig, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6738; shane.romig@dowjones.com [ 13-10-11 2214GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111013e7ad001a4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
