\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Stocks, Bonds Extend Losses In Volatile Session; Merval Down 0.2%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2011\par 23:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks and bonds extended their losses Tuesday, but closed well off intraday lows thanks to a report in the final minutes of trading that European leaders are discussing measures to recapitalize their banks.\par \par \uc2 The Merval index of leading shares finished 0.2% lower at 2,286.73 on volume of 70.5 million pesos ($16.8 million) as a rally minutes ahead of the close erased nearly all of the session's losses.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks also staged an impressive late recovery to close back in the black. The catalyst was a report that European Union finance ministers are looking at how to coordinate the recapitalization of financial institutions.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Global X FTSE Argentina 20 ETF\uc2 , which tracks shares of Argentine companies listed on international markets, fell 1.4% to close at $9.31 in New York.\par \par \uc2 Banks and utilities, the sectors most heavily represented on the Merval, were the most actively traded.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Banco Macro SA\uc2  (BMA, BMA.BA) fell 2.3% to ARS8.65; \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) rose 3.8% to ARS3.79; electric energy holding company \uc2 Pampa Energia SA\uc2  (PAM, PAMP.BA) fell 1% to ARS2.04; and phone company \uc2 Telecom Argentina SA\uc2  (TEO, TECO2.BA) dropped 1.5% to ARS16.20.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS582.50 million, accounting for about 58% of the total volume of securities traded on the exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 The peso-denominated 2033 discount bond shed 4.3% to close at ARS110.00, to yield 14.41%.\par \par \uc2 The Bogar 2018 fell 6.4% in price terms to close at ARS160.50, yielding 22.89%. The Bonar X closed 3.4% lower at ARS358.50, yielding 11.84%.\par \par \uc2 The Boden 2015 closed 3.7% lower at ARS373.75, yielding 10.79%.\par \par \uc2 The 2035 peso-denominated gross domestic product warrant fell 3.1% to ARS13.18, while dollar GDP warrants shed dropped 2.8% to close at ARS63.00.\par \par \uc2 The peso closed unchanged at ARS4.2045 to the U.S. dollar on Argentina's MAE \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  wholesale market.\par \par \uc2 The Central Band of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other public-sector institutions have stepped up their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent weeks to keep the peso steady ahead of presidential and congressional elections on Oct. 23.\par \par \uc2 Puente brokerage said in its afternoon \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the peso steady offering $500 million during the session, though in the end it sold only $140 million.\par \par \uc2 "With the presidential election increasingly near, expectations of any important movement in the exchange rate have become untenable," Puente strategists wrote.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 10-04-11 1730ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111004e7a40014b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
