\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Hochschild CEO Sees Argentina Devaluation Due To Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2011\par 18:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Argentina is where Hochschild is experiencing the most cost inflation\par \par \uc2 --Hochschild fairly unhedged to Argentine peso\par \par \uc2 --Argentine peso devaluation unlikely in 2011\par \par \uc2 (Adds more details throughout beginning in the 1st paragraph.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Alex MacDonald and Ken Parks \par   OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Argentina will have to devalue its peso as inflation drives up the costs of industrial activities like mining, the chief executive of precious metals producer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hochschild Mining PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (HOC.LN) said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Argentina is where inflation is hitting our costs the most, it's not sustainable," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ignacio Bustamante\f28 \uc2 \f2  told Dow Jones Newswires. "There has to be a devaluation," he added, noting the company is keeping its exposure to the Argentine peso fairly unhedged.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a conference call with analysts, Bustamante said he doesn't expect a devaluation to happen this year due to presidential elections in October.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Cristina Kirchner is widely expected to win a second term after she received nearly 51% of the vote in a national primary election held earlier this month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Argentina\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has stepped up its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in recent months to engineer a gradual depreciation of the peso against the U.S. dollar to help exporters, but not so much as to spook Argentines who still see the dollar as a safe haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The peso was recently quoted trading on the local wholesale \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market at ARS4.1830/dlr, bringing its year-to-date depreciation to 4.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But annual gains in consumer prices that most economists say exceed 20% have caused the peso to appreciate in real terms against the greenback. At the same time, Argentina's powerful trade unions have won salary increases of close to 30% this year to compensate for inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hochschild said Tuesday that unit costs per ton of ore mined rose 37% for its Argentine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , far outpacing a 12% on average rise at its lower cost Peruvian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unit costs at its San Jose mine in Argentina rose 37% on year to $191.3/ton due to local price inflation of between 25% to 30% owing mainly to higher wages.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices for raw materials, most notably fuel, steel and reagent, have also risen, the company said, reiterating that it still expects to keep increases to its Argentine unit cost between 25%-30% for the full year in 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Excluding royalties, unit costs rose 33% on year in Argentina during the first half, according to the company.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London-listed Hochschild operates five gold and silver mines in Latin America: three underground mines in Peru, one underground mine in southern Argentina, and an open pit mine in northern Mexico.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The San Jose mine produced 5.2 million of attributable silver equivalent ounces in the first half of 2011, resulting in $156 million in revenue and operating profit before administration, exploration and other expenses of $79.6 million or 28% of the company's total equivalent operating profit during the first half of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hochschild's shares were recently up 0.6% or 2.6 pence at 460.60p.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Alex MacDonald and Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 (0)20 7842 9328; alex.macdonald@dowjones.com [ 23-08-11 1640GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110823e78n0019m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
