\par\par \b \uc2 Argentine Stocks Rally With International Markets; Merval +5%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 548 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2011\par 23:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks joined in Thursday's global equity rally, as upbeat corporate earnings and positive U.S. jobs data emboldened investors battered by steep losses earlier in the week.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval stock index rose nearly 5% to close at 2949.01 points, trimming its year-to-date loss to 5%. Volume totaled 55.3 million pesos ($13.3 million).\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Global X FTSE Argentina 20 ETF\uc2 , which tracks shares of Argentine companies listed on international markets, rose 2% to close at $12.89 in New York.\par \par \uc2 U.S. and European markets rallied on Thursday after technology bellwether Cisco Systems (CSCO) issued upbeat guidance and weekly U.S. jobless data showed a minor improvement.\par \par \uc2 Bull Market Brokers senior analyst Juan Jose Vazquez said that local stocks could get a boost if President Cristina Kirchner fails to garner more than 40% of the vote in primary elections this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 "As long as we see a recovery in the [major international] markets, with a knock-on effect in Argentina, and the scenario I've described playing out in the primaries I would be very positive" on stocks, he said.\par \par \uc2 Kirchner, whose populist policies have long spooked investors, is widely favored to win a second-term in October's presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 Sunday's vote is expected to be a sort of dry run for Kirchner and challengers from the opposition as nearly all the major parties have selected their candidates. To avoid a run-off in November, the winning candidate has to receive at least 45% of the vote or a minimum of 40% and a 10-point lead over the runner-up.\par \par \uc2 Banking group \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) rose 6.5% to ARS5.54; electric energy holding \uc2 Pampa Energia SA\uc2  (PAMP.BA, PAM) rose 5.7% to ARS2.21; aluminium maker \uc2 Aluar Aluminio Argentino\uc2  SA (ALUA.BA) rose 5.3% to ARS4.16; and phone company \uc2 Telecom Argentina SA\uc2  (TEO, TECO2.BA) closed 3.1% higher at ARS20.30.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market totaled ARS652.3 million, accounting for about 64% of the total volume of securities traded on the exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 The 2018 Bogar closed 0.9% higher in price terms at ARS215, yielding 10.62%. The Bonar X fell 0.5% to ARS394.50, yielding 8.88%.\par \par \uc2 Peso-denominated 2035 GDP Warrants rose 2.3% to close at ARS15.86, while dollar-denominated 2035 GDP warrants dipped 0.1% to end at ARS74.45.\par \par \uc2 The peso closed slightly weaker at ARS4.1585 to the U.S. dollar on the MAE local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  wholesale market, compared with ARS4.1570 at Wednesday's close.\par \par \uc2 Puente brokerage said in its afternoon note to clients that trading in the wholesale \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was more subdued Thursday, with $575 million changing hands, about $150 million less than Wednesday's session.\par \par \uc2 According to Puente, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a net buyer during the session. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to ensure a gradual depreciation of the peso versus the greenback.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740;\par \par \uc2 ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 11-08-11 2116GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110811e78b001n0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
