\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Capital Flight Picks Up Ahead Of October General Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2011\par 01:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Businesses and individuals pulled money out of Argentina at a faster pace during the second quarter ahead of presidential and congressional elections in October, according to a measure of capital flight published by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The formation of assets abroad by the nonfinancial private sector rose to $6.13 billion in the quarter, from $3.68 billion in the previous quarter and $2.24 billion in the fourth quarter of 2010, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina said in its quarterly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  report Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  attributed the increase to the "habitual behaviour of the private sector in pre-election periods" with most of the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand coming from small savers.\par \par \uc2 Argentines frequently turn to the U.S. dollar during an election year and 2011 is proving to be no exception.\par \par \uc2 President Cristina Kirchner, whose populist economic policies have long spooked foreign investors, is still widely favored to win re-election this year even after her ruling coalition suffered big losses in local elections in Buenos Aires City and Santa Fe Province last month.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to engineer a gradual depreciation of the peso versus the dollar to control inflation and to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it made net \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of $2.40 billion during the quarter, down from $3.15 billion purchased in the first quarter. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international reserves totaled $50.31 billion on Thursday, compared with $51.7 billion at the end of June.\par \par \uc2 The peso has weakened about 4.2% against the dollar so far this year, closing at ARS4.1535 versus the dollar Thursday. The government has pledged an average exchange rate of around ARS4.10 per dollar in 2011.\par \par \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  said in a note that it expects the peso to end the year at ARS4.25 per dollar as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pension fund agency Anses likely continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  imposes restrictions on capital inflows and outflows, with Argentine residents limited to $2 million a month in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases.\par \par \uc2 Dividends and profits sent abroad by companies totaled $1.66 billion in the quarter, compared with $361 million in the first quarter and $914 million in the fourth quarter of 2010, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Foreign direct investment rose to $829 million in the quarter, totaling $2.56 billion for the 12-month period ended June 30. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that mining, petrochemicals, and construction were the single biggest recipients of that investment.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 04-08-11 2347GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110804e784001n6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2