\par\par \uc2 Top Stories\par \b \uc2 Argentina Official, Parallel  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Rates Gap To Widen\b0\par\par\uc2 Ken Parks  \par \uc2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 731 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2011\par 15:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top News & Commentary\par \uc2 DJCOMM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -Gap between official and parallel rates expected to widen on economic doubts\par \par \uc2 -Parallel rate is a gauge of confidence in Argentina's peso and the economy\par \par \uc2 -The government has sought to strangle trading in the parallel market\par \par \uc2 The gap between Argentina's official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate and a parallel market has narrowed in recent weeks, but traders warn it will likely widen again as investors fear a swifter depreciation of the peso as authorities struggle to address growing imbalances in the economy.\par \par \uc2 "It's a leading indicator on confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate," said Siobhan Morden, a fixed-income strategist at RBS Global Banking & Markets, in reference to the parallel market. "This is actual sentiment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because we can't see the actual sentiment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market because it's so heavily manipulated."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to gradually weaken the peso to help exporters. The peso was recently quoted trading at ARS4.1026/dollar on interbank markets by \uc2 ICAP Plc\uc2 , bringing its year-to-date loss to 3.1%.\par \par \uc2 The parallel rate is the difference between the purchase price of a local stock or bond in pesos and the implied exchange rate when that asset is sold offshore for U.S. dollars. Rates can vary considerably depending on the liquidity of the security used in the transaction, which is known as the blue-chip swap.\par \par \uc2 The trade is a perfectly legal way for individuals and businesses to get money out of Argentina, though at a substantial premium.\par \par \uc2 After reaching levels above ARS4.40/dollar in May, parallel rates have firmed to around ARS4.25 today. Traders attributed the narrowing to government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the bond market and renewed investor appetite for emerging market assets following recent steps to help heavily indebted Greece.\par \par \uc2 Still, the respite could prove short lived amid doubts about economic policy after October's presidential election. President Cristina Kirchner is expected to win a second term.\par \par \uc2 "It's obvious that the market believes in, and is prepared to pay for, a bigger gap. The government's fire power means that its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 " counter the pressure on the parallel rate, said a local trader who asked not to be named.\par \par \uc2 Argentines still see the dollar as a benchmark \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  given their country's long history of financial crisis and violent devaluations. The government has pledged a stable peso this year, with an average exchange rate of around ARS4.10/dollar.\par \par \uc2 "It doesn't impact the real economy and isn't a concern," Deputy Economy Minister Roberto Feletti told reporters last week when asked about the parallel market.\par \par \uc2 But the parallel rate to some degree reflects investor expectations that a weaker peso is in the cards as well as skepticism that Kirchner will adopt more orthodox policies to tame rampant inflation.\par \par \uc2 Most private-sector estimates put annual inflation above 20% due to excessive fiscal spending, an overheated economy and an expanding money supply. Inflation as measured by the government's consumer price index was 9.7% at the end of May.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakening of the peso at a slower rate than inflation has caused the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate in inflation-adjusted terms versus the dollar, to the detriment of some exporters.\par \par \uc2 But a faster nominal depreciation of the peso would only aggravate inflation and capital flight.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, a measure of capital flight tracked by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to $3.68 billion in the first quarter, from $2.24 billion in the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 Trading volumes attributed to the blue-chip swap have dwindled to between $30 million and $50 million a day from more than $200 million two years ago due to a crackdown by regulators, said a trader who spoke on the condition of anonymity.\par \par \uc2 Among other measures aimed at discouraging the trade, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2008 required that investors who buy domestic securities have to wait at least three days before selling them offshore.\par \par \uc2 "The only reason why people are afraid of talking about this is the... government is trying to stop it. But there is nothing illegal about buying and selling bonds," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCOMM1120110711e77b0015q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
