\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Argentina Slams Brazilian Retaliation In Trade Dispute\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shane Romig  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2011\par 01:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina lashed out against its top trading partner Thursday, after Brazil slapped retaliatory trade barriers on Argentine auto parts and vehicle imports.\par \par \uc2 "This kind of behavior is an attack on the normal flow of communication between the two largest members of the Mercosur [trade block]," Industry Minister Debora Giorgi said in a statement Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The Mercosur customs union includes Brazil, Argentina, Paraguay and Uruguay as full members.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's move flies in the face of "the commitments made by the two presidents to balance bilateral trade and achieve peaceful industrialization between two countries," Giorgi said.\par \par \uc2 On May 10, Brazil changed auto imports from "automatic" licensing to "nonautomatic" licensing, which can result in delays at the border for up to 60 days while the country processes the corresponding paperwork, according to a Brazilian government source.\par \par \uc2 The move by Brazil, the world's fourth-largest automobile market, appears targeted at Argentina, the leading source of vehicle imports to Brazil. More than 50% of all imported cars sold in Brazil are shipped from Argentina, according to car maker association Anfavea.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's measures will affect 50% of the bilateral trade between Argentina and Brazil, Giorgi said.\par \par \uc2 Argentina is especially incensed by Brazil springing the measures on them by surprise.\par \par \uc2 "When Argentina imposed the nonautomatic licences on 200 goods last February, the government informed its Brazilian peers 10 days before the official announcement," Giorgi said.\par \par \uc2 Almost 24% of the goods, calculated by value, imported from Brazil face import barriers from Argentina, local economic research firm Abeceb.com said in a report Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Argentina has stepped up import barriers to protect local industry and preserve its still hefty trade surplus.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's attempts to block Brazilian imports during the global crisis in 2009 also provoked a trade spat between the two South American nations. Brazil retaliated with similar measures against Argentine goods, mainly agricultural products and car parts.\par \par \uc2 In the end, diplomacy prevailed and commerce between the two neighbors has since prospered, though Argentina ran a trade deficit of more than $4 billion with Brazil in 2010, according to Abeceb.com.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's latest trade measures affect one of Argentina's largest manufacturing sectors that has benefited in recent years from government financial support as well as strong demand for vehicles and parts in Brazil. Abeceb.com said that 80% of Argentina's vehicle exports and 65% of the auto part exports go to Brazil, with about $7 billion worth of goods in play.\par \par \uc2 Another significant issue affecting bilateral trade are the steep gains in Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to the dollar lately. At the same time, \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the greenback.\par \par \uc2 "It would appear that a strong real is feeding into cheaper imports, which are putting domestic Brazilian firms under pressure," Win Thin, head of emerging markets strategy at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 , said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "It's clear that policy makers are reacting to complaints from domestic auto-parts manufacturers that are getting squeezed by the strong real. Exports have gotten more expensive, while domestic sales are getting squeezed by cheaper imports," Thin wrote.\par \par \uc2 The strong real, together with high labor, materials and transportation costs, have made imported automobiles more attractive to Brazilian consumers. More than 20% of cars sold in Brazil are imported, up from 7% just five years ago.\par \par \uc2 -By Shane Romig, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6738; shane.romig@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Paulo Winterstein in Sao Paulo contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 12, 2011 19:54 ET (23:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110512e75c007md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
