\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina's Stocks End Flat, Bonds Recover To Close Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2011\par 23:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks closed little changed on light volume Thursday, while government bonds attracted buyers after several days of selling.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval stock index fell 1.33 points to close at 3375.96 on volume of 30.2 million pesos ($7.4 million).\par \par \uc2 "While the decline was minimal, it was another sign of the continuing discontent and uncertainty on the local market. The government's decision to put directors representing the state in companies continues to generate selling pressure, though less than in recent days," financial research firm Portfolio Personal said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's pension-fund agency, Anses, has started to exercise its full voting rights in 42 publicly traded companies in which it holds stakes, naming directors to the boards of several companies in recent days. A presidential decree earlier this month abolished a 5% cap on Anses' voting rights.\par \par \uc2 Anses became the country's largest institutional investor after the government nationalized the private-pension system at the peak of the 2008-2009 global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 In the case of banks, \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) and \uc2 Banco Patagonia SA\uc2  (BPAT.BA), the government said late Wednesday that it obtained the consent of other shareholders to name a director to the board of each company even though Anses' stakes didn't automatically entitle it to board representation.\par \par \uc2 Critics said the government is trying to exert further control over the private sector, which is already subject to price controls and punitive regulatory measures.\par \par \uc2 Galicia fell 0.6% to ARS5.44 and Patagonia closed 0.4% lower at ARS15.40, while electric utility \uc2 Pampa Energia SA\uc2  (PAM, PAMP.BA) rose 0.4% to close at ARS2.55.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Global X FTSE Argentina 20 ETF\uc2 , which tracks the shares of Argentine companies listed on international markets, rose 0.7% to US$15.02 in New York.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS475.9 million, equivalent to 83% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants rose 0.6% to ARS17.3290, while U.S. dollar-denominated gross domestic product warrants with the same maturity rose 1.3% to close at ARS73.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-denominated 2017 Bonar X closed 0.6% higher at ARS386, to yield 8.43%. The 2018 Bogar fell 0.5% to ARS210.50, pushing the yield up to 10.19%.\par \par \uc2 The peso-denominated 2033 discount bond rose 0.6% to ARS161, to yield 9.19%. The U.S. dollar-denominated Boden 2015 rose 0.3% to close at ARS407.50, yielding 7.36%.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0800 to the dollar on interbank markets, slightly firmer than the ARS4.0850 at Wednesday's close.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with a bias toward a gradual depreciation to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 28-04-11 2142GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110428e74s0022k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
