\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina's Stocks, Bonds Dip Ahead Of Fed; Merval -0.4%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2011\par 23:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks and bonds on Monday logged modest losses, as investors awaited the results of the U.S. Federal Reserve's monetary policy meeting later in the week.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval stock index closed nearly 0.4% lower at 3,401.16 points on volume of 32.4 million pesos ($9.8 million). Argentina's financial markets were closed last Thursday and Friday.\par \par \uc2 "It's a wait and see game. Buy on weakness and sell on strength. I don't see a lot of enthusiasm in the market right now," said a Mexico City trader. "The international backdrop isn't propitious to go long at this moment."\par \par \uc2 Banking concern \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) fell 1.2% to close at ARS5.66; aluminium maker \uc2 Aluar Aluminio Argentino\uc2  SA (ALUA.BA) closed 0.4% lower at ARS5.40; and electric utility \uc2 Pampa Energia SA\uc2  (PAMP.BA) slipped 0.4% to ARS2.51.\par \par \uc2 Among gaining issues, phone company \uc2 Telecom Argentina\uc2  (TEO, TECO2.BA) closed 1.1% higher at ARS19.15.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Global X FTSE Argentina 20 ETF\uc2 , which tracks the shares of Argentine companies listed on international markets, closed nearly unchanged at $15.20 in New York.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS340 million, equivalent to 40% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants closed 0.2% lower at ARS17.67, while U.S. dollar-denominated gross domestic product warrants with the same maturity rose 0.6% to ARS72.90.\par \par \uc2 The peso-denominated 2033 discount bond closed 0.2% lower at ARS168, to yield 8.74%. The U.S. dollar-denominated Boden 2015 fell 0.2% to close at ARS405.25, yielding 7.45%.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0825 to the dollar on interbank markets, slightly weaker than the ARS4.0780 at Wednesday's close.\par \par \uc2 Puente \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said $434 million changed hands on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during the session, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying $65 million to stabilize the peso.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with a bias toward a gradual depreciation to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 25-04-11 2125GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110425e74p000or\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
