\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Steps Up  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  On Soy Harvest\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2011\par 20:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Energy Service\par \uc2 NRG\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent the peso from strengthening amid an influx of U.S. dollar inflows from the country's soybean harvest.\par \par \uc2 "Last week and the first days [of this week], we are seeing average daily operating levels of $100 million. That is probably going to increase in coming weeks," Carlos Lizer, director at local brokerage Puente, said in reference to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "As of now, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is surely going to have a more important presence in the market," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. dollar, but with a depreciating bias to help domestic exporters.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to purchase $12.5 billion in dollars in the exchange markets this year to build its reserves and weaken the peso. In the first quarter, it bought about $2.7 billion, including $1.4 billion in March alone.\par \par \uc2 Those dollar purchases are set to rise in the coming weeks as grain exporters repatriate the proceeds of their sales from the current soy harvest that runs from March to July.\par \par \uc2 This year through April 8, grain companies repatriated $5.33 billion, according to Ciara-Cec, an industry trade group that represents grain exporters and edible oil producers.\par \par \uc2 As of Thursday, farmers had harvested about 45% of the estimated 49.2 million metric tons of soybeans they are expected to produce this season, according to the Buenos Aires Cereals Exchange.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now buying close to the daily equivalent of soybean sales," Gustavo Quintana, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Lopez Leon Brokers, said. "In recent days, it has been buying more than $100 million a day."\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0675/dollar on interbank markets Thursday, bringing its year-to-date loss against the greenback to 2.2%, following a 4.5% decline in 2010.\par \par \uc2 President Cristina Fernandez's administration, which heavily influences the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina, has set an average exchange-rate target of around ARS4.11 and a year-end rate of ARS4.15 for 2011.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's so-called managed float of the peso has allowed Argentina to buck the trend of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation in several of its top trading partners in South America during the last two years.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real has firmed about 5.4% versus the dollar so far this year, even in the face of a growing number of capital controls put in place by Brazilian policy makers. Chile has had more success than Brazil in taming its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  managing to weaken the Chilean peso 1.1% after it appreciated 7.8% in 2010.\par \par \uc2 However, the expansion of the money supply as a direct result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is contributing to inflation that most private-sector economists say has become anchored north of 20%.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman wasn't immediately available for comment.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been careful to limit the peso's gradual slide to avoid fanning inflation even more by making imported goods more costly.\par \par \uc2 Greater private-sector demand for dollars closer to general elections in October should help the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meet its $12.5 billion target without overshooting, said Lizer, who thinks the peso will likely end the year slightly weaker than official forecasts.\par \par \uc2 "We estimate the dollar at ARS4.25 to ARS4.30 and an average of...ARS4.15 to ARS4.20," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740; ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 04-15-11 1449ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument NRG0000020110415e74f0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
