\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina's Stocks Gain As Investors Unfazed By Govt Decree\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 545 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2011\par 23:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks closed slightly higher on Wednesday, as investors shrugged off a presidential decree that will greatly increase government control over many of the country's biggest publicly traded corporations.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval stock index recovered from mild losses early in the session to close nearly 0.2% higher at 3,407.24 points on volume of 39.9 million pesos ($9.8 million).\par \par \uc2 A presidential decree published on Wednesday removed limits on the government's ability to vote the shares it owns in dozens of companies. Pension agency Anses holds stakes in 42 local companies after the government nationalized the private pension system at the peak of the 2008-2009 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Prior to the decree, the government's voting rights were capped at a 5% equity stake even if its actual ownership in a firm exceeded that level. The abolition of that limit greatly enhances the government's influence, especially in the 15 companies in which its ownership exceeds 20%.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think its going to be a worry. Of course its not good, but companies have had [Anses on board] since 2008," said Brian Joseph, head stock and bond trader at local brokerage Puente.\par \par \uc2 Banking concern \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) rose 2.3% to close at ARS5.72; \uc2 Banco Macro SA\uc2  (BMA, BMA.BA) rose 1.2% to ARS17.40; and shares of \uc2 BBVA Banco Frances SA\uc2  (BFR, FRAN.BA) rose 2.5% to ARS16.50.\par \par \uc2 Among declining issues, phone company \uc2 Telecom Argentina\uc2  (TEO, TECO2.BA) closed 1.2% lower at ARS20.90.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Global X FTSE Argentina 20 ETF\uc2 , which tracks the shares of Argentine companies listed on international markets, rose 0.3% to $15.32 in New York.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS655 million, equivalent to 60% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants rose 1.1% to ARS17.67, while U.S. dollar-denominated gross domestic product warrants with the same maturity closed 0.2% higher at ARS73.30.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-denominated 2017 Bonar X closed 0.2% higher at ARS383.10, to yield 8.37%. The 2018 Bogar rose 0.3% to ARS224.75, yielding 7.77%.\par \par \uc2 The peso-denominated 2033 discount bond closed 1.5% higher at ARS172.50, to yield 8.37%. The U.S. dollar-denominated Boden 2015 rose 0.4% to close at ARS404, yielding 7.33%.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0600 to the dollar on interbank markets, slightly weaker than the ARS4.0575 at Tuesday's close.\par \par \uc2 Puente \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said $920 million changed hands on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during the session, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying $45 million to stabilize the peso.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with a bias toward a gradual depreciation to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 13-04-11 2118GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110413e74d001bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw