\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Stocks Slip On US Growth Worries, Bonds Gain\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks and Shane Romig  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2011\par 23:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks closed lower on Tuesday, tracking losses in global markets as worries about U.S. economic growth and Japan's nuclear crisis curbed investor appetite for equities.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval stock index fell 1.4% to close at 3,401.55 points with volume a low 40 million pesos ($10 million).\par \par \uc2 U.S. and European equity markets closed firmly lower following a disappointing outlook by aluminium giant \uc2 Alcoa Inc\uc2 . (AA) and data showing that U.S. trade activity softened in February. Japan's decision to upgrade the severity of its nuclear crisis to the same level as the 1986 Chernobyl disaster did little to boost investor sentiment.\par \par \uc2 That weighed on the banking sector in Argentina. Shares of banking group \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) fell 2.1% to close at ARS5.59; shares of \uc2 Banco Patagonia SA\uc2  (BPAT.BA) fell 0.4% to ARS5.16; and shares of phone company \uc2 Telecom Argentina\uc2  (TEO, TECO2.BA) closed 0.7% lower at ARS21.15.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Global X FTSE Argentina 20 ETF\uc2 , which tracks the shares of Argentine companies listed on international markets, fell 1.5% to $15.27 in New York.\par \par \uc2 While investors turned their backs to Argentine stocks, strong growth prospects this year and high yields spurred bond buying.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants rose 0.4% to ARS17.48, while U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants with the same maturity closed 0.3% higher at ARS73.1480.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-denominated 2017 Bonar X closed 0.5% higher at ARS396.50, to yield 8.38%. The 2018 Bogar rose 0.1% to ARS224, yielding 7.85%.\par \par \uc2 The peso-denominated 2033 discount bond closed 1.7% lower in price at ARS170, to yield 8.52. The U.S. dollar-denominated Boden 2015 rose 0.3% to close at ARS402.50, yielding 7.38%.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0575 to the dollar on interbank markets, slightly weaker than the ARS4.0525 at Monday's close. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade volume totaled $650 million.\par \par \uc2 The peso trended higher through much of the day, but the government stepped in late to buy $60 million and push the peso down, local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders Puente said in a market note.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with a bias toward a gradual depreciation to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 12-04-11 2136GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110412e74c0019m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
