\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Argentina To Keep Capital Controls In Place--Minister\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks and Alberto Messer  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2011\par 05:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina has no plans to lift capital controls any time soon as developing nations struggle to contain the destabilizing effects of a world awash in liquidity, according to a top government minister.\par \par \uc2 "We have controls on short-term capital flows, speculative capital. We are going to keep them," Economy Minister Amado Boudou told Dow Jones Newswires at the sidelines of an event Friday night.\par \par \uc2 For years, Argentina has required that foreigners deposit 30% of any financial investments they bring into the country in a zero-interest account at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a year.\par \par \uc2 Argentine policymakers say those controls have been instrumental in keeping hot foreign money out of the country. Critics say it has discouraged foreign investment in the country's tiny domestic capital market.\par \par \uc2 The low interest rates in developed countries and lax monetary policy at the Federal Reserve have spurred investors to pour their money into commodities and the higher-yielding financial assets of fast-growing economies in Asia and Latin America.\par \par \uc2 That wave of speculative capital has led to the rapid appreciation of a number of emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with serious implications for global trade.\par \par \uc2 In Latin America, Brazil has steadily expanded capital controls to halt the appreciation of the real and its detrimental effect on exporters, while Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January launched a $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program aimed at weakening the peso.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  has enjoyed considerable success in weakening its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even as grain exporters who are enjoying high prices repatriate billions of dollars of proceeds from the sale of their crops.\par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina's managed float of the peso with a bias toward depreciation has come at the cost of greatly expanding the money supply as the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  prints pesos to sop up those incoming dollars.\par \par \uc2 A booming economy that is widely expected to expand more than 6% this year coupled with an increase in the money supply are fueling inflation that most private sector economists say is north of 20%.\par \par \uc2 The heavily criticized national statistics agency's most recent reading of annual inflation as measured by its consumer price index was 10% at the end of February.\par \par \uc2 An International Monetary Fund technical assistance mission is in Argentina April 4-11 to assist the agency, Indec, in developing a new national CPI amid widespread distrust of official data.\par \par \uc2 "We hope to have a report soon and will look at the possibility of putting into practice a national price index as soon as possible," Boudou said.\par \par \uc2 The administration of President Cristina Fernandez stunned investors last November by inviting the IMF to help Indec improve the way it measures inflation in a move that signaled a rapprochement between Argentina and the multilateral lender follows years of icy relations.\par \par \uc2 Fernandez had long vilified the IMF, whose free-market policies in the 1990s, she says, crippled Argentina's industrial sector and contributed to its $100 billion sovereign-debt default in 2001.\par \par \uc2 The IMF and Argentina have also been at odds over the latter's refusal to submit to a periodic review of its economic policies, a so-called Article IV surveillance consultation, that is required of all IMF member nations.\par \par \uc2 Boudou will travel to Washington DC for the IMF's spring meeting April 15-17.\par \par \uc2 Argentina and other developing nations have pushed for deep reforms to the institution, which has historically demanded wrenching economic reforms of countries that have sought its financial aid.\par \par \uc2 Boudou said recent comments by the head of the IMF to the effect that the so-called Washington Consensus of the 1990s--which called for deregulated markets and privatizations--is a thing of the past are significant.\par \par \uc2 "It's indispensable that the IMF staff be profoundly changed because that is where the rancid idea that there is only one policy for the world is rooted," he said in reference to overhauling the IMF.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks and Alberto Messer, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740; ken.parks@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 08, 2011 23:33 ET (03:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110409e749000nd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
