\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Stocks, Bonds Close Higher; Merval +0.2% For The Week\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 987 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2011\par 23:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks and bonds closed higher on low volume Friday, as investors largely ignored a looming U.S. government shutdown and a sharp jump in oil prices.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval stock index rose 0.7% to close at 3480.39 points on volume of 41.1 million pesos ($10.1 million). The Merval rose 0.2% this week, bringing its loss so far this year to 1.2%.\par \par \uc2 U.S. markets closed lower amid rising oil prices and the specter of a government shutdown as the country's two political parties struggled to reach a deal on the federal budget. Meanwhile, European markets posted respectable gains after debt-strapped Portugal said it would seek financial aid from the European Union.\par \par \uc2 Shares of banking group \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) rose 1.4% to ARS5.80; shares of electric utility holding company \uc2 Pampa Energia SA\uc2  (PAMP.BA) closed 1.6% higher at ARS2.48; and shares of oil company \uc2 Petrobras Argentina SA\uc2  (PZE, PESA.BA) dipped 1.5% to ARS9.65.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Global X FTSE Argentina 20 ETF\uc2 , which tracks the globally listed shares of Argentine companies, rose 1.2% to US$15.72 in New York.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS495.3 million, equivalent to 80% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 Delphos Investment said in an afternoon note to clients that it sees room for more gains in inflation bonds linked to the CER index, which tracks the government's heavily criticized consumer price index.\par \par \uc2 "We continue to see the Bogar 18 as the most attractive instrument to bet on a continuation of the rebound," Delphos strategists wrote.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-denominated Bonar X closed 0.2% higher in price at ARS394.80, yielding 8.46%. The Bogar 2018 rose 0.5% to ARS224, yielding 7.84%.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants rose 0.8% to ARS17.39, while U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants with the same maturity rose 0.4% to ARS73.50.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0525 to the dollar on interbank markets, slightly stronger than Thursday's close of ARS4.0550.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "High commodities prices are supporting exporters' activity and the pace of private U.S. dollar outflow is falling. [The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] continues to rebuild international reserves and promote a gradual depreciation of the peso in nominal terms," \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  strategists said in a note.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740; ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 08-04-11 2108GMT ]\par \par \uc2 (The item "Argentina Stocks, Bonds Close Higher; Merval +0.2% For The Week" published at 2108 GMT Friday incorrectly stated in graph 9 the closing levels for peso and U.S. dollar GDP warrants.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Ken Parks \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks and bonds closed higher on low volume Friday, as investors largely ignored a looming U.S. government shutdown and a sharp jump in oil prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark Merval stock index rose 0.7% to close at 3480.3