\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Stocks, Bonds Rally With Global Markets; Merval +1.7%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 21:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks and bonds rallied on Monday, as investor sentiment got a boost from receding nuclear fears in Japan and a multibillion-dollar telecommunications deal in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval stock index rose 1.7% to close at 3332.95 points on volume of 42.9 million pesos ($10.6 million). Even so, the Merval is still down 5.4% for the year.\par \par \uc2 Stock markets across Europe and the Americas logged strong gains as investors cheered Japan's success over the weekend in controlling the damaged Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant north of Tokyo, even as Western powers stepped up their military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in oil-producing Libya.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  on Monday raised its forecast for expansion of Argentina's gross domestic product to 6.8% this year from 5.6% previously. Argentina's economy is booming, but at the expense of double-digit inflation that private-sector economists say is close to 25%.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0425 to the dollar on interbank markets, slightly weaker than Friday's close of ARS4.0400.\par \par \uc2 "The supply [of dollars] by exporters and private investors equaled demand by private entities, which kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the sidelines during the trading session," local brokerage Puente said in an afternoon note to clients.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with a bias toward a gradual depreciation to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 Shares of banking group \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) rose 0.6% to ARS5.46; shares of electric utility holding company \uc2 Pampa Energia SA\uc2  (PAMP.BA, PAM) closed 1.3% higher at ARS2.40; \uc2 Petrobras Argentina SA\uc2  (PZE, PESA.BA) jumped nearly 5.8% to ARS9.55; and aluminium maker \uc2 Aluar Aluminio Argentino\uc2  SA (ALUA.BA) rose 3% to ARS5.48.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Global X FTSE Argentina 20 ETF\uc2 , which tracks the globally listed shares of Argentine companies, rose 2.3% to $14.75 in New York.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS505.5 million, equivalent to 79% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-denominated Bonar X closed 0.8% higher in price at ARS383, yielding 9%. The 2018 Bogar rose 2% to ARS216.25, yielding 8.91%.\par \par \uc2 The peso-denominated 2033 discount bond rallied 2.5% in price to close at ARS164, to yield 8.88%. The U.S. dollar-denominated Boden 2015 rose 0.9% to close at ARS405.50, yielding 7.97%.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants rose 1.1% to ARS15.90, while euro-denominated GDP warrants with the same maturity rose 0.6% to ARS79.50.\par \par \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said Monday it continues to recommend Argentina's GDP warrants, especially those denominated in euros which have the greatest theoretical upside.\par \par \uc2 "While the instruments have been extremely volatile in the past, they have withstood the recent selloff well, suggesting that investors are more comfortable with valuations and Argentina's growth outlook," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The warrants pay if real GDP growth is above a certain threshold. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  strategists said the warrants seem close to being "in the money," as real GDP growth is well above the threshold, which implies a large payout.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 21-03-11 2050GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110321e73l00172\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
