\par\par \b \uc2 Argentine Stocks, Bonds Extend Gains For Second Day; Merval +0.5%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 548 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 22:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks and bonds logged modest gains and the peso was steady on Friday, as local assets continued to recover from a selloff earlier in the week triggered by Japan's nuclear crisis.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval stock index rose 0.5% to close at 3277.87 points on volume of 36.5 million pesos ($9.03 million). The Merval lost 3.7% this week, bringing its year-to-date loss to nearly 7%.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0400 to the U.S. dollar on interbank markets, unchanged from Thursday's close.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with a bias toward a gradual depreciation to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 "Domestic assets continue to be 'tied' to the external climate...responding to volatile headlines from multiple global risks of which Japan and the Middle East have captured the greatest attention in recent days," economic research firm Estudio Ber said in a note.\par \par \uc2 U.S. and European markets rose for a second day on Friday after the United Nations authorized a no-fly zone in Libya and Japan continued its struggle to bring a damaged nuclear power plant under control.\par \par \uc2 The 9.0-magnitude earthquake and ensuing tsunami on March 11 devastated northeastern Japan and led to explosions and radiation leaks at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant.\par \par \uc2 The natural disaster in the world's No.3 economy coupled with political unrest in the Middle East dented investor appetite for stocks and emerging market assets at the start of the week.\par \par \uc2 Argentina has been somewhat shielded from the volatility in international financial markets thanks to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's managed float of the peso and capital controls that have deterred foreign investment in the local stock market. Indeed, retail investors are the main driver of trading on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange.\par \par \uc2 Shares of banking group \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) rose 0.2% to ARS5.43; shares of phone company \uc2 Telecom Argentina SA\uc2  (TEO, TECO2.BA) rose 1.5% to ARS19.90; and shares of diversified manufacturer and sugar maker Ledesma SA (LEDE.BA) jumped 3.7% to ARS9.16.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Global X FTSE Argentina 20 ETF\uc2 , which tracks the globally listed shares of Argentine companies, rose 1% to finish at $14.52 in New York.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS587.4 million, equivalent to 75% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-denominated Bonar X closed 0.4% higher at ARS380, yielding 9.15%. The 2018 Bogar rose 0.7% to ARS212, yielding 9.50%.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-denominated Boden 2015 rose 0.45% to close at ARS401.75, yielding 8.20%.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants rose 0.9% to ARS15.73, while U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants with the same maturity rose 1% to ARS63.40.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 18-03-11 2140GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110318e73i001em\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal33