\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina's Stocks, Bonds Track Global Markets Lower On Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 17:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks and bonds came under selling pressure Tuesday, with the benchmark Merval equity index down about 4% in mid-day trading, following along with a Japan-led plunge by global markets.\par \par \uc2 The Merval was down 4% at 3,222.67 points at 11:48 a.m. EDT on volume of 32.8 million pesos ($8.1 million).\par \par \uc2 Juan Jose Vazquez, senior analyst at Bull Market Brokers, said major global stock markets were due for a pullback following recent gains.\par \par \uc2 "I think there are some buying opportunities [locally]. I would recommend building [positions in stocks] amid the declines, but keeping some money on the sidelines in case the market falls even further," he said.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0325 to the U.S. dollar on interbank markets, slightly weaker than the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ARS4.0300 close on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the dollar, but with a bias towards a gradual depreciation to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 U.S. and European markets traded deep in the red after Japan's benchmark Nikkei Stock Average finished 10.6% lower on mounting concerns that radiation might leak from nuclear power plants damaged by last week's earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's direct economic ties to Japan are limited, with trade between the two nations totaling just $2.05 billion last year, according to Argentine government data.\par \par \uc2 Capital goods and related parts and accessories accounted for 62% of Argentina's $1.19 billion in Japanese imports that year, while raw materials and agriculture products represented 79% of the $858 million in goods that Argentina exported to the Asian nation.\par \par \uc2 Commerce with Japan was minimal when compared to the $124.94 billion in foreign trade Argentina posted in 2010. Far more important to Argentina's economy are Brazil and China. Trade with those two countries last year totaled $32.51 billion and $13.86 billion, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Shares of banking group \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) were off 5.24% at ARS5.43; shares of phone company \uc2 Telecom Argentina SA\uc2  (TEO, TECO2.BA) were down 3.6% at ARS19.00; shares of electric energy holding company \uc2 Pampa Energia SA\uc2  (PAM, PAMP.BA) were 4.6% lower at ARS2.30; and shares of the country's largest oil and gas producer \uc2 YPF SA\uc2  (YPFD.BA, YPF) were down 8.4% at ARS196.\par \par \uc2 YPF shares were stung by falling oil prices and Monday's decision by controlling shareholder \uc2 Repsol YPF\uc2  (REP.MC) to sell up to 7.1% of the Argentine company through American Depositary Shares.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Global X FTSE Argentina 20 ETF\uc2 , which tracks the globally listed shares of Argentine companies, was recently down 3.2% at $14.20 in New York.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS112.9 million, equivalent to 64% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 The peso-denominated 2033 discount bonds were down 1.8% at ARS157.19, yielding 9.23%. The 2018 Bogar was off 1% at ARS211.25, yielding 9.60%.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-denominated Boden 2015 was trading 0.9% lower at ARS400, yielding 8.22%.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants were down 1.7% at ARS15.63, while U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants with the same maturity were off 1% at ARS63.50.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 15-03-11 1611GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110315e73f001bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
