\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina's Stocks Tumble With Global Markets On Europe, Libya\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 22:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks on Thursday posted their biggest drop in a month as Europe's smoldering sovereign-debt crisis and fighting in Libya prompted investors to seek the perceived safety of the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval stock index fell 1.5% to close at 3,405.92 on volume of 43.5 million pesos ($10.8 million).\par \par \uc2 U.S. and European stock markets closed sharply lower after \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Investors Services downgraded Spain's sovereign credit rating and fighting in oil-rich Libya between government and rebel forces intensified.\par \par \uc2 "Local equities have been following the price of oil. We think oil has hit a top and we are reducing our exposure to energy and oil-related companies as a short-term trade," said Brian Joseph, head stock and bond trader at local brokerage Puente.\par \par \uc2 Joseph said that long-term he is still bullish on energy shares like Brazil's \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  SA (PBR) and steel-tube maker \uc2 Tenaris SA\uc2  (TS) as well as Argentine banking stocks.\par \par \uc2 Shares of electric utility holding company \uc2 Pampa Energia SA\uc2  (PAM, PAMP.BA) closed 2.4% lower at ARS2.48; shares of banking concern \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) fell 0.8% to ARS5.89; and shares of phone company \uc2 Telecom Argentina SA\uc2  (TEO, TECO2.BA) fell 2.7% to finish at ARS19.95.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS504.6 million, equivalent to 58% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-denominated Bonar X closed 0.4% lower at ARS380.50, yielding 9.03%. The U.S. dollar-denominated Boden 2015 dropped 0.3% to close at ARS403.80, yielding 7.95%.\par \par \uc2 The 2018 Bogar lost 0.6% to close at ARS220.70, yielding 8.10%.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants finished 0.3% lower at ARS64.75.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0325 to the U.S. dollar on interbank markets, unchanged from the close on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with a bias towards a gradual depreciation to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740,\par \par \uc2 ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 10-03-11 2111GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110310e73a001gr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
