\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina's Stocks End Lower; Bonds Rise In Light Trading\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 21:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks finished lower while bonds eked out modest gains Wednesday, in a session marked by light trading volumes amid a holiday-shortened week.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval stock index closed 0.3% lower at 3,457.52 on volume of 37.9 million pesos ($9.40 million). Argentina's financial markets were closed Monday and Tuesday in observance of Carnaval.\par \par \uc2 "I don't see big moves this week. A static market on low volume," Dionisio Corneille, a trader at local brokerage Corneille Agente de Bolsa, said.\par \par \uc2 "We are going to move with the [big international] markets, especially Brazil. Local factors aren't going to influence" the local market that much, he said in reference to trading during the second half of the month.\par \par \uc2 Shares of electric utility holding company \uc2 Pampa Energia SA\uc2  (PAM, PAMP.BA) closed 1.2% lower at ARS2.54; shares of banking concern \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) fell 0.3% to ARS5.94; and shares of diversified industrial group Ledesma SA (LEDE.BA) slipped 2.2% to finish at ARS8.80.\par \par \uc2 Among gaining issues, shares of \uc2 Petrobras Argentina SA\uc2  (PESA.BA, PZE) rose 5% to ARS9.80, and shares of phone company \uc2 Telecom Argentina SA\uc2  (TEO, TECO2.BA) closed 0.5% higher at ARS20.50.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS442.7 million, equivalent to 46% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-denominated Bonar X closed 0.5% higher at ARS382, yielding 8.93%. The U.S. dollar-denominated Boden 2015 closed 0.1% higher at ARS404.85, yielding 7.86%.\par \par \uc2 The 2018 Bogar fell 0.3% to ARS222.00, yielding 7.82%.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants rose 2.3% to close at ARS15.93, while U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants with the same maturity also finished 1.5% higher at ARS64.95.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0325 to the U.S. dollar on interbank markets, slightly weaker from Friday's close of ARS4.0300.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with a bias towards a gradual depreciation to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740; ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 09-03-11 2050GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110309e739001fu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
