\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina's Stocks, Bonds Tumble On Middle East; Merval Down 1.4%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2011\par 22:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks and bonds tumbled on Wednesday, as political unrest in Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries member nation Libya and rising oil prices dented appetite for emerging market assets.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval index fell 1.4% to close at 3429.08 points on volume of 118.1 million pesos ($29.3 million). Wednesday marked the fifth consecutive session that the Merval closed in the red, bringing its year-to-date loss to 2.7%.\par \par \uc2 "Volatility is going to continue. Everything depends on what happens in the Middle East. Problems over there affect the U.S. and therefore end up hitting us directly and indirectly," said Agusto Farina, an equity trader at Buenos Aire-based brokerage Amirante Galitis.\par \par \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) shares fell 2.9% to ARS6.04; mortgage lender \uc2 Banco Hipotecario SA\uc2  (BHIP.BA) shares fell 5% to ARS2.65; electricity distribution utility \uc2 Edenor SA\uc2  (EDN, EDN.BA) shares fell 5.2% to ARS2.18; and electricity transmission firm Transener SA (TRAN.BA) shares lost 2.8% to end at ARS1.38.\par \par \uc2 The session's few gaining shares included \uc2 Banco Macro SA\uc2  (BMA, BMA.BA), which rose 0.3% to ARS17.30, and the country's largest oil and gas producer, \uc2 YPF SA\uc2  (YPF), whose shares rose 1.9% to ARS212.00.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS641.4 million, equivalent to 62% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-denominated Bonar X closed 0.2% lower at ARS369.75, yielding 9.57%. The peso-denominated 2033 discount bond plunged 5.4% to ARS163.75, pushing the yield up to 8.80%.\par \par \uc2 The 2018 Bogar fell 3.2% to ARS221.6570, yielding 7.83%.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants fell 1.7% to close at ARS15.33, while U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants with the same maturity finished 1.3% lower at ARS62.20.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0300 to the U.S. dollar on interbank markets, slightly weaker than Tuesday's close of ARS4.0275.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with a bias towards a gradual depreciation to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 23-02-11 2112GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110223e72n001eh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
