\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Tells Uruguay New Import Barriers Won't Hurt Trade\b0\par\par\uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 2011\par 02:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- Argentine authorities sought to reassure Uruguay on Monday that a new set of import barriers thrown up by Argentina isn't targeted toward the neighboring country and won't affect trade between the two.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's trade secretary, Eduardo Bianchi, called his Uruguayan counterpart, Sebastian Torres, on Monday to explain that imports from Uruguay "won't have problems getting into the country" and offered to set up a bilateral commission to monitor imports, Argentina's production ministry said in a press release.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Argentina expanded to about 600 from 400 the number of imported goods that have to request a Non Automatic License, affecting items such as luxury automobiles, consumer electronics, and glass and metal products.\par \par \uc2 That immediately set off alarm bells among manufacturers in Uruguay and Brazil, Argentina's top trade partner. Washington Burghi, president of Uruguay's leading industrial trade group, the CIU, criticized the move in Uruguayan daily El Pais on Friday. In 2009, Uruguay ran a trade deficit of $1.3 billion with Argentina.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturers in Brazil voiced similar complaints, and Brazilian Trade Minister Fernando Pimentel visited Buenos Aires on Friday to seek assurances that trade wouldn't be disrupted. Following the meeting, Argentine Industry Minister Deborah Giorgi said the expansion of the list of products that require an NAL was aimed at "unfair competition" from countries located outside of the Mercosur customs union, which includes Argentina, Brazil, Uruguay and Paraguay.\par \par \uc2 The administration of President Cristina Fernandez has tried to boost local industry and job creation through formal and informal trade barriers. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  also regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to foster a gradual peso depreciation to keep export-oriented manufacturers competitive and protect the local industry from imports. According Giorgi, those policies allowed Argentina to substitute $9.2 billion in imports during the last year and has saved 1 million jobs.\par \par \uc2 "The NALs have been applied in sectors where the country produces enough to satisfy demand and where there have been investments where it's strategic to preserve their development," Monday's press release said.\par \par \uc2 Trade between Argentina and Brazil--South America's two biggest economies--has grown tenfold in the last two decades to nearly $33 billion in 2010, although Argentina posted a $3.38 billion trade deficit with Brazil last year.\par \par \uc2 However, the trade relations between the two neighbors haven't been free of the occasional spat. In 2009, Argentina raised barriers against its top two trading partners, Brazil and China, in an effort to protect local manufacturers during the global economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 Brazil retaliated with similar measures against Argentine goods, mainly agricultural products and car parts. In the end, diplomacy prevailed and commerce between the two neighbors has since prospered, helping to fuel steep growth. Exports to Brazil have helped to underpin Argentina's booming economy, which President Fernandez said Friday expanded 9.1% last year.\par \par \uc2 Argentina is a major buyer of Brazilian tractors, automobile parts and iron ore, while Brazil imports large quantities of automobiles from Argentina and buys about 90% of its neighbor's wheat exports.\par \par \uc2 Trade barriers have also angered China in the past, and it retaliated by refusing to import Argentine soyoil for six months in 2010. That spat was eventually smoothed over, with China resuming soyoil imports and pledging to loan Argentina about $9.5 billion to upgrade its railway network and buy Chinese-made railroad equipment.\par \par \uc2 However, Argentina has continued to take actions to protect local manufacturers against Chinese imports. On Thursday, Giorgi signed a decree renewing antidumping measures against Chinese-made air-conditioning units.\par \par \uc2 -By Shane Romig, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740; shane.romig@dowjones.com [ 02-21-11 2037ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110222e72m00005\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
