\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Argentina Stocks Close Higher Led By Bank Shares; Merval +1.4%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks  \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2011\par 22:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks closed higher on Monday, as investors bid up local bank shares after two major financial institutions reported strong results last week.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval index rose 1.4% to close at 3508.93 points on volume of 56.1 million pesos ($13.9 million).\par \par \uc2 Adrian Mayoral, a fixed-income and equity trader at Mayoral Bursatil stock exchange, said the expiration of local options on Thursday and quarterly earnings reports will likely set the tone for a market that is trading close to record highs.\par \par \uc2 "Earnings have the potential to confirm these price levels" or trigger profit-taking, he said in a telephone interview.\par \par \uc2 Shares of local bank \uc2 Banco Macro SA\uc2  (BMA, BMA.BA) rose 4% to ARS18.05, crosstown rival BBVA Banco Frances (BFR, FRAN.BA) rose 1.6% to ARS16.05, and Grupo Financiero Galicia (GGAL, GGAL.BA) closed 2% higher at ARS6.24.\par \par \uc2 Aluminum maker \uc2 Aluar Aluminio Argentino\uc2  (ALUA.BA) rose 2.7% to ARS5.82, telecommunications firm \uc2 Telecom Argentina\uc2  (TEO, TECO2) climbed 1.4% to ARS21.45, while shares of electric utility holding company \uc2 Pampa Energia\uc2  (PAM, PAMP.BA) fell 0.8% to ARS2.64.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS280.8 million, equivalent to 51% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 The peso-denominated 2033 discount bond fell 1.2% to ARS172, yielding 8.30%. The U.S. dollar-denominated Boden 2015 closed 0.5% higher at 392.50, yielding 8.52%.\par \par \uc2 The 2018 Bogar fell 1.1% to ARS229.90, yielding 6.65%.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants closed 1.3% higher at ARS15.55, while U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants with the same maturity rose 0.9% to ARS63.25.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0250 to the U.S. dollar on interbank markets, slightly weaker than Friday's close of ARS4.0225.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with a bias towards a gradual depreciation in order to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 "[The peso] is depreciating more than we have anticipated on the back of a more-aggressive [\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] approach. We will remain neutral on the peso until we see more concrete signs of consolidations and officials not that aggressive in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," \uc2 BNP Paribas\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said in a note Monday.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740,\par \par \uc2 ken.parks@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 14, 2011 16:22 ET (21:22 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110214e72e006cy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
