\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina's Stocks, Bonds Down On Emerging-Market Weakness\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks and Taos Turner   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2011\par 22:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks and bonds closed sharply lower on Thursday as investors appeared to shift away from emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval index fell 2.6% to close at 3414.06 points on volume of 66.8 million pesos ($16.6 million).\par \par \uc2 "What we're seeing is that a flight of funds from emerging markets in general. This is not just a problem for Argentina," said Dionisio Corneille, a trader at a brokerage carrying his family name. "On top of that, Argentine utility companies and the energy sector have been hit by new government fines."\par \par \uc2 The power distributor Edenor (EDN.BA) led the declines, falling 5.85% to ARS2.25.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday Planning Minister Julio De Vido said the government would fine the country's top three power distributors for allowing blackouts to occur in late December. Edenor will have to pay ARS22 million.\par \par \uc2 The power transmitter Transener (TRAN.BA) fell 4.54% to ARS1.47 while \uc2 Pampa Energia SA\uc2  (PAMP.BA) slid 2.57% to ARS2.65.\par \par \uc2 Banking group \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) fell 4.1% to ARS6.07; shares of telecommunications firm \uc2 Telecom Argentina SA\uc2  (TEO, TECO2.BA) fell 2.6% to ARS20.85; and mortgage lender Banco Hipotecario (BHIP) dropped 5.6% to close at ARS2.88 after it reported a big drop in fourth-quarter net profit.\par \par \uc2 Corneille said some investors have also become a bit uneasy about the government's new effort to control money laundering by cracking down on financial transactions and credit-card use. The measures have left some investors wondering how far the government will go to regulate financial transactions.\par \par \uc2 Given the small size of Argentina's market, any pressure from sellers is usually enough to push the market down.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS330.5 million, equivalent to 57% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 The peso-denominated 2033 discount bond fell 2.5% to ARS176, yielding 8.00%. The U.S. dollar-denominated Boden 2015 closed 0.3% lower at ARS388.75, yielding 8.74%.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants closed 0.3% lower at ARS15.45, while U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants with the same maturity declined 0.6% to ARS62.40.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0200 to the U.S. dollar on interbank markets, slightly weaker than Wednesday's close of ARS4.0180.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with a bias towards a gradual depreciation in order to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 Volume in the market totaled $651 million.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 10-02-11 2122GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110210e72a001rv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
