\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina's Stocks, Bonds Log Modest Gains; Merval Up 0.2%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Februar 2011\par 22:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks and bonds closed with modest gains on Wednesday as a better-than-expected reading for U.S. employment and the temporary lifting of a damaging port strike in this country's grain belt buoyed investor sentiment.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval index rose nearly 0.2% to close at 3640.98 points on volume of 49.8 million pesos ($12.4 million).\par \par \uc2 Brazil's Ibovespa stock market fell 1.7% and the Dow Jones Industrial Average closed nearly unchanged as clashes in Egypt offset a positive report on U.S. private-sector job creation.\par \par \uc2 Helping local assets was the Argentina government's decision on Tuesday night to order strikers to end their blockade of key grain ports clustered along the banks of the Parana River while union leaders and employers try to reach an agreement on wage demands during a cooling off period of 15 business days.\par \par \uc2 The blockade that started last Wednesday involved workers indirectly linked to the loading terminals and crushing plants located at the ports, which handle a significant portion of Argentina's soymeal and soyoil exports to foreign markets. Crushing plant and port workers returned to their jobs Wednesday as the striking unions lifted their siege.\par \par \uc2 "There wasn't a lot of trading happening today. The session was somewhat erratic, bouncing in and out of positive and negative territory," said a Buenos Aires trader. "The U.S. jobs report on Friday is going to be very important."\par \par \uc2 Grain exporter \uc2 Molinos Rio de la Plata SA\uc2  (MOLI.BA) jumped 5.1% to ARS38.90, aluminium maker \uc2 Aluar Aluminio Argentino\uc2  SA (AAARF, ALUA.BA) rose 1.5% to ARS6.10, and banking group \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) closed 0.6% higher at ARS6.48.\par \par \uc2 Among declining issues, shares of sugar maker and diversified manufacturing group Ledesma SA (LEDE.BA) fell nearly 1% to ARS10.15, and shares of oil and gas company \uc2 Petrobras Argentina SA\uc2  (PZE, PESA.BA) fell 3.2% to ARS10.45.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS518.8 billion, equivalent to 69.3% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar-denominated 2017 Bonar X bond rose 0.2% to ARS368.60, yielding 9.42%. The U.S. dollar-denominated Boden 2015 rose 0.2% to ARS392.60, yielding 8.37%.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants closed 1% higher at ARS15.5980, while U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants with the same maturity rose 0.9% to ARS64.15.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0175 to the U.S. dollar on interbank markets, compared with ARS4.0100 at Tuesday's close.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the greenback, but with a bias towards depreciation.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 02-02-11 2131GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110202e722001c7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
