\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina's Stocks, Bonds Fall As Egypt Unsettles Global Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2011\par 23:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's stocks and bonds fell on Friday, tracking declines in global markets, as civil unrest in Egypt and the resulting jump in oil prices gave investors plenty of reasons to seek the perceived safety of commodities and U.S. Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Merval index closed 0.9% lower at 3616.79 points on volume of 46.2 million pesos ($11.5 million). The index lost 1.8% during the week, although it's still up 1.7% so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Stocks fell in neighboring Brazil, Argentina's Number 1 trading partner, with the Ibovespa index closing nearly 2% lower, while U.S. markets also posted steep losses as demonstrations in Egypt against the regime of Pres. Hosni Mubarak extended into a fourth day.\par \par \uc2 Shares of electricity holding company \uc2 Pampa Energia\uc2  (PAM, PAMP.BA) fell 1.7% to ARS2.85, banking group Grupo Financiero Galicia (GGAL, GGAL,BA) shed 1.2% to finish at ARS6.45, and cross-town rival \uc2 Banco Patagonia SA\uc2  (BPATL, BPAT.BA) fell 3.5% to ARS5.50. Banks and utility shares logged some of the biggest gains on the Merval last year.\par \par \uc2 Turnover on the local bond market was ARS365.2 billion, equivalent to 71.8% of the total volume of securities traded on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange during the session.\par \par \uc2 The peso-denominated 2033 discount bond fell 2% to ARS187.60, yielding 7.38%. The Boden 2015 dipped 1.5% to ARS386, yielding 8.70%.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants closed 0.3% lower to ARS15.31, while U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants with the same maturity fell 2.7% to ARS63.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted by Thomson Reuters closing at ARS4.0025 to the U.S. dollar on interbank markets, compared to ARS3.9950 at Thursday's close. The session marked the peso's biggest slump against the dollar in months.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight band relative to the greenback.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 28-01-11 2211GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110128e71s001lv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
