\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Argentina Prepared To Recognize $9B Paris Club Debt - Report\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2011\par 15:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )--Argentina is prepared to recognize that it owes the Paris Club of sovereign creditors nearly $9 billion, but will seek a repayment period of up to six years, local daily Pagina 12 reported on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Citing unnamed government sources, Pagina 12 said the administration of President Cristina Fernandez will focus on obtaining favorable repayment terms, five to six years compared to the 18 months demanded by the Paris Club, rather than disputing the amount it owes.\par \par \uc2 The $9 billion includes unpaid principal on loans as well as interest and penalties accrued after Argentina defaulted on its debts with private and sovereign creditors in 2001, according to the paper, which is close to the government.\par \par \uc2 Economy Ministry officials weren't available for comment.\par \par \uc2 Finance Secretary Hernan Lorenzino is leading a technical mission to Paris this week to hammer out an agreement on the total amount Argentina owes the Paris Club.\par \par \uc2 Economy Minister Amado Boudou said Monday that he was optimistic that a repayment plan could be reached by June.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's outstanding debt was $6.87 billion at the end of 2009, according to the Paris Club's annual report for that year. Most of that is owed to Germany, Italy, Japan, the U.S. and the Netherlands.\par \par \uc2 Settling its debts with the informal group of creditor nations would reopen Argentina's access to financing and loan guarantees provided by the international financing agencies of Paris Club members.\par \par \uc2 The Fernandez administration appears to have softened earlier demands that a deal include the forgiveness of penalties and interest. Argentina has already won a major concession from the club, which waived its long standing requirement that the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  participate in the process. \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bitterly opposed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \uc2 IMF\uc2  in any debt talks with the club.\par \par \uc2 However, Argentina might have a difficult time convincing the club that it should be granted a lengthy repayment schedule given its economy grew an estimated 9% last year and the government's ability to tap the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $52 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to pay debt.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month the Economy Minister borrowed $7.5 billion of reserves from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pay private creditors.\par \par \uc2 Coloring the negotiations are general elections in October, in which Fernandez is widely expected to seek a second four-year term. With that in mind, the administration might be unwilling or unable to take on significant new debt servicing commitments at the same time it ramps up spending on infrastructure projects and social programs ahead of the elections.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 26, 2011 09:58 ET (14:58 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110126e71q003h7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
