\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ SURVEY: Argentina's Nov Industrial Production Seen Rising 8.1%\b0\par\par\uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2010\par 21:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Industrial Production (Year-On-Year): \par               Nov    Oct    Sep    Nov09   FY/2009 \par Forecast:    +8.1%  +8.9%  +9.0%    -        - \par Actual:        -    +8.2%  +9.7%  +1.1%    +0.4% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Ken Parks\f28 \par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's industrial output likely expanded at a brisk pace in November as manufacturers benefited from buoyant domestic demand and exports, according to economists.\par \par \uc2 "The industrial sector continues to be supported by solid external demand, particularly from Brazil. But its competitiveness has been gradually eroded by the fact that costs are growing faster than the nominal depreciation of the peso," \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Alberto Ramos said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The national statistics agency, Indec, is expected to report that industrial production rose 8.1% from November 2009, according to the median forecast of nine economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Indec is scheduled to release its report on Friday at 2 p.m. EST.\par \par \uc2 Local industry is benefiting from an economy that the government says will expand 9% this year. Argentina's largest trading partner, Brazil, is also a key export market for manufactured goods.\par \par \uc2 The automobile industry, a key manufacturing segment, produced 76,813 vehicles last month, a 36% increase from the year-ago period. Exports rose 26.4% on the month to 49,740 units.\par \par \uc2 At the same time that manufacturers are seeing robust demand for their products, they also face what many economists say is double-digit inflation that is pushing up input and labor costs. Earlier this year, unions in many sectors negotiated salary increases of between 20% and 40%.\par \par \uc2 Indec reported Wednesday that annual inflation as measured by the consumer price index was 11% at the end of November, compared to private-sector estimates of well over 20%.\par \par \uc2 The credibility of Indec's economic statistics has been called into question since former President Nestor Kirchner made personnel changes at the agency in 2007.\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices will likely rise even more in 2011, with the latest monthly survey of inflation expectations published on Wednesday by the respected Torcuato di Tella University showing Argentines expect consumer prices to rise 30% during the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 For exporters, rising domestic costs haven't been fully offset by the exchange rate. The peso has depreciated just 4.5% versus the U.S. dollar so far this year due in large part to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's regular \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market aimed at gradually weakening the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Indeed, many economists see a stable exchange rate as one of the few mechanisms keeping inflation from accelerating even further in the face of profligate government spending, a consumer spending boom fed by wage rises, and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has aggressively increased the money supply.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 15, 2010 15:10 ET (20:10 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101215e6cf005ca\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
