\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Markets Reel Amid Global Selloff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shane Romig   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2010\par 22:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentine stock and bond markets plunged Tuesday as a global selloff spurred by concerns over Chinese growth and a likely rescue package for struggling Ireland.\par \par \uc2 The Merval Index of leading shares slumped 4.52% to close at ARS3,122.70, pulling the Merval down to its lowest level in two weeks. The loss was the biggest percentage point drop since early May when the European debt crisis first rattled markets. However, the index is still up 35% on the year. Volume reached 116 million pesos ($29 million).\par \par \uc2 "The drop in international markets pulled Argentina down [today]. Tomorrow we'll have to see what happens with Ireland and whether the market can return to the upward trend" observed in recent weeks, said Dionisio Corneille, president of the local brokerage bearing his name.\par \par \uc2 European finance ministers are working on an international aid package for Ireland that could total up to EUR100 billion ($135 billion) and include credit from the euro zone and International Monetary Fund, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 It was all gloom across global markets Tuesday. U.S. stocks swooned in their biggest one-day drop in nearly a month as fears mounted over a slowdown in Chinese economic growth and criticism of the Federal Reserve's recent action grew. Fears that China would move to raise interest rates also hit commodities prices on Tuesday, sending crude-oil and grain futures tumbling.\par \par \uc2 In Argentina, the selloff focused on banks, which have posted steep gains in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Mortgage lender \uc2 Banco Hipotecario SA\uc2  (BHIP.BA) fell 5.64% to ARS3.01; banking concern BBVA Banco Frances (BFR, FRAN.BA) slumped 6.06% to ARS15.50, and Grupo Financiero Galicia (GGAL, GGAL.BA) lost 6.41% to close at ARS5.84.\par \par \uc2 Argentine bonds also suffered from the global market pessimism Tuesday, shrugging off the president's announcement Monday night that she planned to start negotiations with the Paris Club of debtor nations to settle up on about $7.5 billion in defaulted debt owed to member countries. President Cristina Fernandez said the Paris Club had agreed to one of Argentina's key demands, that the International Monetary Fund not be involved in the deal.\par \par \uc2 The federal government's benchmark peso-denominated 2033 discount bond closed 2% lower in price terms at ARS171.50, yielding 7.9%.\par \par \uc2 The Boden 2015, considered a good proxy for measuring the appeal of newly issued government bonds or the government's ability to sell them, was 1.21% lower at ARS366, yielding 9.48%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the peso lost ground against the greenback Tuesday. The peso eased to ARS3.975 to the dollar from ARS3.9725. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight range against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 The government has made it clear that it plans to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable through next year. "There's not going to be any problem with the exchange rate in 2011 ... and I mean no problem," Economy Minister \uc2 Amado Boudou\uc2  told Radio La Red Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 -By Shane Romig, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6738; shane.romig@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Ken Parks contributed to this article. [ 16-11-10 2107GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101116e6bg001e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
