\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Could Be Dragged Into  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Fight On Inflation, Brazil\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 936 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2010\par 23:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina has largely sat on the sidelines of the so-called global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war thanks to capital controls and its closely managed float of the peso, but rampant domestic inflation and moves by its biggest trading partner, Brazil, to limit the appreciation of the real could soon pull it into the fray.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina President Mercedes Marco del Pont said earlier this week that capital controls requiring foreigners to deposit 30% of any financial investments they bring into the country in a zero-interest account at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for 12 months have been instrumental in keeping hot foreign money at bay.\par \par \uc2 "We are very calm because what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to buy everyday as it accumulates reserves are commercial dollars, dollars from foreign trade. Speculative capital still hasn't shown up in Argentina," she said in prepared remarks at the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's checkered economic history has also served to discourage both financial and direct investment in the country. Argentina took a hard line with creditors following its default on about $100 billion of sovereign debt in 2001, offering just 33 cents on the dollar in two debt swaps in June this year and in 2005. Like Nestor Kirchner, her recently deceased husband and predecessor as Argentina's head of state, President Cristina Fernandez has also voiced little faith in private enterprise and pursued statist economic policies of price controls and heavy government spending.\par \par \uc2 The low interest rates in developed countries and lax monetary policy at the Federal Reserve have spurred investors to dump U.S. securities in favor of commodities and the higher-yielding financial assets of fast-growing economies in Asia and Latin America.\par \par \uc2 That wave of speculative capital has led to the rapid appreciation of emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with serious implications for global trade.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of G-20 nations, in a summit in South Korea that wrapped up Friday, pledged to move toward more market-determined exchange-rate systems and avoid competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to foster a gradual peso depreciation to keep export-oriented manufacturers competitive and protect the local industry from imports.\par \par \uc2 Currently trading around ARS3.9625 to the dollar, the peso has weakened nearly 13% against the greenback since the start of 2009. President Cristina Fernandez's 2011 budget proposal assumes an exchange rate of ARS4.10 vs. the dollar at the year.\par \par \uc2 However, Mercedes del Pont faces an increasingly difficult balancing act of counteracting the natural tendency of the peso to appreciate amid massive inflows of dollars from soybean and cereal exports, while at the same time trying to keep inflation from accelerating if the peso were to slide too much.\par \par \uc2 For exporters, the gradual weakening of the peso coupled with the appreciation of Brazil's real have proven to be a boon. Exports to Brazil grew 36% on the year to $10.5 billion in the January-September period, led by shipments of industrial goods like automobiles, which surged 42% to $7.28 billion, according to national statistics agency Indec. Even so, Argentina posted a trade deficit of $2.18 billion with Brazil in the first nine months of the year.\par \par \uc2 The real has gained more than 30% against the dollar since early 2009, leading Brazilian policy makers and industry executives to express concern in recent months that a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hurt exports.\par \par \uc2 In October, Brazil raised its financial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  tax on foreign investment in fixed-income securities and a separate tax on margin deposits for derivatives transactions in an effort to curb investment inflows. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil Deputy Governor Luiz Awazu said on Nov. 2 that additional measures might be necessary.\par \par \uc2 For Enrique Mantilla, president of exporters association CERA, exchange rates are less of a concern to exporters than the strength of the Brazilian economy and galloping inflation in Argentina that is pushing up operating costs.\par \par \uc2 "What is most important is that Brazil grows," he said in a telephone interview. "Inflation needs to be better controlled. The rate of inflation today is bad."\par \par \uc2 According to Indec, Argentina's annual inflation was 11.1% at the end of October. However, private sector economists say inflation is actually more than double the official rate as a result of heavy government spending, a booming economy that could grow 9% this year and hefty wage increases negotiated by unions. The respected Torcuato Di Tella university's recent monthly poll showed that Argentines expect annual inflation of 30% during the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 The Fernandez administration, which blames inflation on greedy businessmen and supply bottlenecks resulting from a lack of private investment, regularly denies accusations that it manipulates Indec data.\par \par \uc2 Compared to its main trading partners, Argentina's wages continue to be "competitive," Marco del Pont told senators.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  economist Daniel Volberg said inflation is slowly eroding the competitiveness that Argentine exporters have enjoyed from a weak nominal exchange rate and Brazil's strong real.\par \par \uc2 "There's a question mark about whether Brazil is going to see a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But if they do, depending on the magnitude of the weakness, it could have important repercussions for Argentina both for industrial exporters and also for those industries that have been helped by the weak exchange rate regime to give them protection from import competition," he said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Alberto Messer contributed to this article. [ 12-11-10 2218GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101112e6bc001sr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
