\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Stocks, Bonds Jump On Foreign Inflows; Merval +4.2%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2010\par 22:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's benchmark Merval stock index hit a new record level and government bonds logged solid gains on Thursday as yield-hungry foreign investors pushed local asset prices higher a day after the Federal Reserve announced it would inject $600 billion into the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 The Merval index closed 4.2% higher at 3339.97 points led by bank and energy shares, bringing its year-to-date gains to 44%. Volume totaled a hefty 224.2 million pesos ($56.6 million).\par \par \uc2 "Volume has risen sharply in the last few days. Investors who weren't in the market are jumping in. Foreign flows into Argentina are huge," said a Bueno Aires trader. The trader added that turnover on the stock market in recent days has returned to the levels observed before the government nationalized the private pension system in late 2008.\par \par \uc2 The private pension fund managers, known as AFJPs, used to be Argentina's largest institutional investors until the administration of President Cristina Fernandez seized their assets and folded them into social security agency Anses.\par \par \uc2 The pension grab coupled with the global financial crisis in 2008 and early 2009 caused trading volume on the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange to dwindle. However, with benchmark interest rates at rock-bottom lows across much of the developed world and the Federal Reserve printing dollars in a bid to reflate the U.S. economy, investors looking for attractive returns on their money are taking a fresh look at Argentina.\par \par \uc2 Market sentiment toward Argentina also received a boost from the government's bond swap with investors in June as well as the death last month of former president and political strong man Nestor Kirchner, who wielded considerable influence over economic policy in the government of his wife, President Fernandez. Investors are taking a big bet that a widowed Fernandez will eventually adopt economic policies that are more market-friendly than those advocated by her husband, who backed heavy state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Among local issues, the shares of mortgage lender \uc2 Banco Hipotecario SA\uc2  (BHIP.BA) rose 6% to ARS3.56, shares of local bank \uc2 Banco Patagonia SA\uc2  (BPAT.BA) soared 15.8% to ARS5.88, and shares of banking group \uc2 Grupo Financiero Galicia SA\uc2  (GGAL, GGAL.BA) finished 6.7% higher at ARS6.40. Shares of energy conglomerate \uc2 Pampa Energia S.A\uc2 . (PAMP.BA), whose assets include an electricity distribution and transmission companies, rose 4.5% to ARS2.34.\par \par \uc2 The BNY Mellon Argentina ADR Index, which tracks U.S.-listed shares of Argentine stocks, rose 4.9% to $286.27.\par \par \uc2 The peso weakened against the U.S. dollar, closing at ARS3.96, compared to ARS3.9580 at Wednesday's close. The Cenral Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso trading within a tight range against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  said Thursday that it expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the exchange rate relatively stable "as a depreciation of the peso could trigger capital flight."\par \par \uc2 On the local bond market, the federal government's benchmark peso-denominated 2033 discount bond rose 3.4% to close at ARS177.60, pushing the yield down to 7.53%. Yields move inversely to bond prices.\par \par \uc2 The Global 2017 bond, issued in June as part of the government's debt swap with the holders of defaulted bonds, closed 1% higher at ARS427, yielding 8.11%.\par \par \uc2 The Boden 2015, considered a good proxy for measuring the appeal of newly issued government bonds or the government's ability to sell them, rose 0.8% to close at ARS379, yielding 8.43%.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's 2035 peso-denominated GDP warrants rose 1.7% to ARS12.85, while U.S. dollar-denominated GDP warrants with the same maturity jumped 4.9% to ARS54.75.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4103-6740, ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 04-11-10 2115GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101104e6b4001sl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
