\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  to continue  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2 -cenbanker\b0\par\par\uc2 422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2010\par 22:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief says country's 2011 outlook bright\par \par \uc2 * Country will continue measures to protect economy\par \par \uc2 By Jorge Otaola\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Oct 25 (Reuters) - Argentina will continue to protect its booming economy in 2011 by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, accumulating foreign reserves and controlling capital flows, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Mercedes Marco del Pont has said those mechanisms were adopted as public policies by emerging markets and give Argentina autonomy in the globalized world. [ID: nN02222834]\par \par \uc2 The South American country has long backed state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy to stoke growth and avoid volatility.\par \par \uc2 President Cristina Fernandez often notes that many other nations adopted similar measures after the global financial crisis hit in 2008.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at the Thomson Reuters Economic Forum in Buenos Aires on Monday, Marco del Pont said Argentina's economic outlook is bright.\par \par \uc2 "There's enormous optimism because we know Argentina will continue its high economic growth (in 2011), that it will continue to improve its employment levels and that it will enjoy trade and fiscal surpluses," she told investors and company executives in a teleconference.\par \par \uc2 "And in that context, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to carry on these policies to keep the exchange rate steady, to accumulate reserves and to avoid ... speculative capital inflows," she added.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys or sell dollars on the local market nearly every day to keep the peso <ARSB=> steady and to sustain a competitive exchange rate that helps spur exports.\par \par \uc2 Latin America's third-biggest economy plans to use foreign reserves to pay debt to private investors next year, allowing the state to fund high spending ahead of an October 2011 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Marco del Pont said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's role will be key given the global context "where capital flows are provoking a lot of damage -- not only creating a bubble effect but also appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Argentina is seen growing nearly 9 percent this year, but the government's 2011 budget bill puts next year's expansion at a conservative 4.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The government forecasts the trade surplus at $9.68 billion in 2011, below the $12.73 billion estimated for 2010. Inflation is seen at 8.4 percent in 2011, way below private forecasts.\par \par \uc2 Marco del Pont was confirmed as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head in September through a presidential decree. ($1 = 4.0375 Argentine pesos) (Writing by Luis Andres Henao, Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 ARGENTINA-CENBANK/|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|RNP|DNP|PCO   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101025e6ap001pt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
