\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Emerging mkts know  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2  key -  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 252 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2010\par 17:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details on policies, monetary program revision)\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Sept 2 (Reuters) - Developing countries are aware they must protect their economies by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, accumulating foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and controlling capital flows, Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Such mechanisms have become part of emerging market nations' public policies and "give us autonomy in this globalized world," Mercedes Marco del Pont told a seminar of central bankers and academics.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long backed state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy to foster growth and avoid volatility. President Cristina Fer nandez often notes that many other nations adopted a similar stance after the global financial crisis hit in late 2008.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys or sell dollars on the local market nearly every day to keep the peso <ARSB=> steady and to sustain a competitive exchange rate that helps spur exports.\par \par \uc2 Marco del Pont also defended the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision last week to raise its targets for money supply growth, saying monetary policy needs to better reflect what is happening in the real economy and not be implemented in a vacuum. [ID:nN26213826]\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had said it was revising its monetary program due to much stronger-than-expected economic growth this year, which it forecast at between 8.9 percent and 9.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Hilary Burke and Guido Nejamkis; Editing by Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 LATAM-ECONOMY/ARGENTINA (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100905e69200093\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
