\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Key risks to watch in Argentina\b0\par\par\uc2 By Helen Popper   \par \uc2 906 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2010\par 16:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Feb 5 (Reuters) - Argentina's government is trying to clean up lingering damage from its massive 2002 default and woo back investors so it can sell debt on global markets and ease a financing crunch.\par \par \uc2 President Cristina Fernandez, whose approval ratings have slipped to about 20 percent, faces another tight financing year but she will be reluctant to put the brakes on public spending in the run-up to a 2011 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the issues investors are watching:\par \par \uc2 DEBT SWAP\par \par \uc2 The government says it will soon launch a swap to mop up $20 billion in defaulted bonds, offering investors a steep discount. A prompt and successful completion of the swap, which has been delayed repeatedly, is crucial for Latin America's No. 3 economy to return to global credit markets for the first time in eight years. [ID:nN04130847]\par \par \uc2 A positive outcome could let Argentina sell bonds in global markets at lower rates and would reduce the risk of lawsuits by holders of untendered bonds.\par \par \uc2 If the swap collapses or gets put on hold indefinitely it would likely send Argentine bond prices tumbling. <AR/BONOS> A failure would also worsen a tight financing outlook and could force the government to borrow even more heavily from state agencies or tap \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Rate of acceptance by bondholders. Markets will see a rate of less than 60 percent as disappointing.\par \par \uc2 -- Impact of swap outcome on debt yields and whether they would fall low enough to encourage new bond issues.\par \par \uc2 CASH CRUNCH\par \par \uc2 Argentina faces debt obligations of about $15 billion this year, most of which come due in the second half of the year, and for now it cannot turn to global markets for fresh funds.\par \par \uc2 That will likely mean a deepening of the fiscal deficit, which reached $1.8 billion last year, and could force the government to find ever more creative ways to maintain the primary budget surplus, which measures how much money the government has to make debt payments.\par \par \uc2 Fernandez sought to tap $6.6 billion from foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to help pay debt, but the courts have blocked the plan. She may try to revive it in Congress, or could look for other funding sources such as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  profits. [ID:nN28182949]\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Efforts to revive the reserves fund plan in Congress.\par \par \uc2 -- Deepening of fiscal deficit as debt payments come due.\par \par \uc2 -- Creative accounting to maintain primary budget surplus.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, SOCIAL UNREST\par \par \uc2 A wave of strikes and street protests erupted late last year and demands could increase in 2010, especially if inflation rises and erodes living standards. [ID:nN13365378]\par \par \uc2 Such protests put particular pressure on Fernandez because her eroded support base is mainly concentrated among the poor living on the outskirts of the capital, many of whom have benefited from hikes in welfare payments and pensions.\par \par \uc2 The conflicts also reflect splits in the ruling Peronist party and the trade union movement, which have deepened since the government was defeated in mid-term elections last year.\par \par \uc2 Signs of an economic rebound could help ease unrest, but only if job creation and wage rises outpace inflation.\par \par \uc2 Farmers remain disgruntled with agricultural policy and more hardline leaders have threatened to resume strikes and protests if their demands for lower export taxes are not met.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Resumption of farmer protests, possible impact on key grains shipments.\par \par \uc2 -- Further erosion of government popularity.\par \par \uc2 -- Prolonged unrest that could fuel demand for dollars, capital flight. <ARSB=>\par \par \uc2 -- Any sign government is losing backing of CGT umbrella union and powerful mayors in Buenos Aires suburbs as rival Peronist factions jostle for position ahead of the 2011 election.\par \par \uc2 INFLATION\par \par \uc2 Despite a sharp economic slowdown, inflation clocked 7.7 percent last year and private forecasts put it at twice that. As the economy rebounds, consumer spending is picking up again and many analysts warn inflation will surge this year.\par \par \uc2 Controversy over consumer price data compiled by the INDEC national statistics agency continues. Economy Minister Amado Boudou has vowed to restore creditability to the figures, and the gap between official numbers and private estimates narrowed slightly last year, but that will be far more difficult if inflation picks up.\par \par \uc2 Brisk public spending is not expected to slow in the run-up to next year's presidential vote, stoking inflation.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Gap between official price data and private surveys.\par \par \uc2 -- Any sudden surges that could hit poor and spark unrest.\par \par \uc2 -- Public sector wage demands.\par \par \uc2 PRESSURE ON BUSINESS, INSTITUTIONS\par \par \uc2 Fernandez's government has nationalized several public service companies and is currently threatening to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in leading phone operator \uc2 Telecom \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an anti-trust ruling against parent \uc2 Telecom Italia\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 She has also appointed a close ally as head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which might let the government increase its already tight control over the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nN04152202]\par \par \uc2 Several high-profile court rulings have gone against the government in recent weeks, blocking the use of reserves to pay debt and suspending \uc2 Telecom Italia\uc2 's forced sale of its holdings in \uc2 Telecom Argentina\uc2 . [ID:nN02246176]\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Any changes to \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statutes to cut its autonomy.\par \par \uc2 -- Possible nationalization of \uc2 Telecom Italia\uc2  holdings.\par \par \uc2 -- Government effort to regain influence in the courts. (Editing by Kieran Murray)\par \par \uc2 ARGENTINA-RISKS/ (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|G|RBN|RNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100205e6250029n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
