\par\par \b \uc2 S&P: Weaker Econ, Fiscal Outlook Could Harm Argentina Rating\b0\par\par\uc2 892 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2009\par 22:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentina's government has time to change its economic policies without having to take drastic action, but a weaker economic and fiscal outlook means time is running out, according to ratings agency \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Perhaps the biggest risk for Argentina's rating is that the government might not use the limited capacity it still enjoys for changing economic policies in the coming months," said S&P analyst Sebastian Briozzo in a report published Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 President Cristina Fernandez's ruling party suffered a major setback in June 28 midterm Congressional elections, which many analysts and opposition politicians have said provides an opportunity for the government to change direction, and drop some of its more unorthodox and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.\par \par \uc2 If the government sticks to its guns, which S&P said seems most likely at this stage, it may not be able to control the worsening economic and fiscal outlook, which, from the agency's point of view, could hurt the country's single-B-minus speculative grade sovereign rating.\par \par \uc2 "Deteriorating economic and fiscal performances and the continuing lack of access to voluntary debt markets could put additional pressures on the ratings in the future if corrective actions are not implemented," Briozzo said.\par \par \uc2 The government seems to have its 2009 obligations to private-sector bond investors covered, but there's less certainty about 2010, Briozzo said.\par \par \uc2 The Argentine government must repay about $13.5 billion in the second half of 2009, of which $6 billion is due to private investors, the report said. The government has plenty of room to tap other government sources of cash, such as the pension system, state-owned Banco de la Nacion and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while multilateral lenders "could still add as much of $800 millions to the treasury."\par \par \uc2 For the whole of 2010, the government owes about $13.2 billion, of which $7.2 billion is estimated to be in private hands. It's possible to imagine the government muddling through, Briozzo said, but there are other, more preferable alternatives.\par \par \uc2 "The government's ambition is to be able to access voluntary markets in 2010, but this will depend on improving economic policymaking," he said.\par \par \uc2 'Voluntary' refers to tapping investors through the capital markets to raise funding, rather than by turning to 'involuntary' sources within the government system.\par \par \uc2 Investors will be watching to see whether President Fernandez gives the incoming economy minister, Amado Boudou, a mandate to begin rebuilding relations with international financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Argentina has been unable to turn to overseas funding since its mammoth $100 billion default of late 2001, and a subsequent debt restructuring in 2005. Investors holding about $30 billion in bonds rejected the offer of about 30 cents on the dollar, and have been fighting in court every since.\par \par \uc2 Added to that, the government's relations with the International Monetary Fund have soured, blocking a plan to settle defaulted debts with the Paris Club group of government creditors, another obstacle to overseas funding.\par \par \uc2 Compounding these problems are allegations that since 2007 the government has interfered directly in the government statistics institute, or Indec, primarily to manipulate inflation data. Indec says inflation is running at about 5.5% per year, while private-sector economists say the real figure could be three times that number.\par \par \uc2 Settling the dispute at Indec would quickly help restore links with the IMF and Paris Club, which would be a "positive shock" for expectations about \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy, S&P said.\par \par \uc2 "Despite how emblematic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Indec has become, making the institute more transparent should not be considered a very ambitious task," Briozzo said. "Reforming the inflation index should be easier now that private sector and official estimates of inflation have started to converge because of the economic slowdown in Argentina."\par \par \uc2 As well as these concerns over sources of financing, the analyst said there are two other possible default scenarios which have to be considered.\par \par \uc2 The first is that the government launches a liability management \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to exchange existing debt, possibly of the Boden 2012 bonds, which, depending on the terms, S&P "could interpret as a distressed exchange and therefore characterize as a default."\par \par \uc2 The second possibility is that the government makes another grab for cash along the lines of the seizure of the private-sector pension funds at the end of 2008.\par \par \uc2 "Although it is difficult to predict what types of measures those could be, what seems more likely is that these potential actions, taken in a context of deteriorating political support, declining economic activity, and vulnerability to capital outflows, could end up accelerating financial instability," Briozzo said.\par \par \uc2 "Drastic and unanticipated measures...could lead to a run on deposits and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would set the basis for a default on the government debt, despite the modest debt payments due over the medium term," Briozzo said.\par \par \uc2 While Argentina may be struggling at home, the improving global economy, particularly growth in China, India and, most importantly, neighboring Brazil, should help.\par \par \uc2 "If Brazil continues to enjoy [economic] growth, it will improve the regional environment in which Argentina operates and provide opportunities for the country's business sector," Briozzo said.\par \par \uc2 -By Matthew Cowley, Dow Jones Newswires; +54 11 4103 6740; matthew.cowley@dowjones.com [ 07-08-09 1632ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020090708e578000ij\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
