\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Argentine inflation not top worry but still needles\b0\par\par\uc2 By Hilary Burke   \par \uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2008\par 16:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Dec 30 (Reuters) - Although inflation worries have lost ground to recession fears since Argentina's booming economy hit the skids several months ago, the problem of rising prices won't vanish next year.\par \par \uc2 Annual inflation of around 20 percent this year is seen falling by between 5 and 10 percentage points in 2009, with one optimistic analyst forecasting a single-digit increase.\par \par \uc2 Consumer spending and investment are cooling fast as a global downturn breeds caution among everyday Argentines and business executives. Sinking prices for the country's commodities have eased inflation pressures but also thinned the once-padded pockets of the country's farmers.\par \par \uc2 "While many forces point to a reduction in inflation next year, namely lower global commodities prices and lower activity levels, inflation persistence in the form of wage pressures and the peso's depreciation will still keep inflation high," said Sebastian Vargas, the Argentina economist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Growth in Latin America's No. 3 economy averaged 8.8 percent a year from 2003 through 2007 but is seen slowing to 7.0 percent this year. The economy will grow at much lower rates in 2009, and could even contract, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 High growth has been accompanied by high inflation, obscured by accusations of systematic data manipulation at the national statistics office, INDEC.\par \par \uc2 Consumer groups, economists and even some state statisticians have accused the government of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in INDEC to under-report inflation, both for political gain and to lower payments on inflation-adjusted debt.\par \par \uc2 Officials strenuously deny any tampering, but government figures are widely discredited.\par \par \uc2 INDEC reported 7.9 percent annual inflation through November <ARECI09>, with private estimates near 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 Marina Dal Poggetto, director of consulting group Estudio Bein & Asociados, expects annual inflation to fall to 12 percent next year from around 19 percent in 2008.\par \par \uc2 While prices for tradable goods have stagnated, the cost of services -- such as private education and health care -- will rise in direct proportion to wage hikes next year, which she said are unlikely to dip below 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Inflation a little above 10 percent is not a major problem. The country will be focused on other priorities, but we won't have price stability either," Dal Poggetto said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  DILEMMA\par \par \uc2 One of the factors that could aggravate inflation next year is the continued depreciation of the Argentine peso <ARSB=>.\par \par \uc2 "Inflation should fall ... but the risk is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciates faster than expected or the government pursues a dangerously expansive macroeconomic policy," said Bertrand Delgado of New York-based IDEAglobal consultancy, who forecasts 2009 inflation at between 12 percent and 15 percent.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market nearly every day to avert abrupt changes in the peso's value. Local industry leaders want a weaker peso since neighbors such as Brazil and Chile have let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall sharply against the dollar amid the global downturn.\par \par \uc2 But in a country hit by a freez on bank deposits and a massive devaluation in 2002, analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot permit a steep depreciation without sparking further capital flight -- and stoking inflation.\par \par \uc2 "If fiscal pressures emerge, one possible way to close a potential fiscal and current account gaps would be to let the peso depreciate more rapidly, which would stoke inflation and allow the government to continue to collect a large 'inflation tax' from the economy," \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  said earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 The people who would pay the highest price in this scenario are the poor, who make up about one-third of Argentina's 40 million people, according to private estimates.\par \par \uc2 "Rising unemployment and high inflation is the worst mix the poorest segment of the population can have," Vargas said.\par \par \uc2 ARGENTINA-ECONOMY/INFLATION (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|U   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081230e4cu000xl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
