\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Argentina industrial output growth slows in October\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lucas Bergman   \par \uc2 461 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2008\par 19:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with comment from economy minister, head of industrial business group; adds byline)\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Nov 24 (Reuters) - Argentina's industrial production rose just 2.6 percent in October from a year earlier and fell from September, hurt by a sharp slowdown in the automotive sector, the government said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The year-on-year figure came in well below the 4.9 percent median forecast in a Reuters poll of eight analysts, whose non-seasonally adjusted estimates ranged from 2.4 percent to 5.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 The automotive sector, one of the main drivers of industrial growth in the last five years, grew 6.2 percent in October from a year earlier, but saw output fall 7.8 percent compared with September.\par \par \uc2 Growth in that sector topped 22 percent in the first 10 months of the year when compared with the same period of 2007.\par \par \uc2 Several carmakers have started cutting back workers' hours and are negotiating lower wages with unions to try to avoid firing people as demand for vehicles starts to shrink.\par \par \uc2 The textile industry also took a beating in year-on-year terms, sliding 10.4 percent, while output in rubber and plastics fell 10.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 Overall factory output fell 1.9 percent from September, according to the government's seasonally adjusted data.\par \par \uc2 The global financial crisis has started to cool the Argentine economy and analysts are now forecasting a sharp slowdown, or possibly even a recession, next year.\par \par \uc2 Economy Minister Carlos Fernandez on Monday rejected the possibility of a recession, however, and said growth may just slow from previous high rates.\par \par \uc2 Latin America's No. 3 economy grew by at least 8.5 percent in each of the last five years and is seen expanding about 7 percent in 2008.\par \par \uc2 Business executives gathered at an annual meeting of the Argentine Industrial Union, or UIA in Spanish, urged the government to help revive the sector's competitive edge, focusing particularly on the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The head of the UIA, Juan Carlos Lascurain, reiterated that the Argentine peso should be allowed to depreciate further against the dollar, saying "we need to resolve this in light of how other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region have moved."\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Brazil and Chile have fallen much more sharply against the dollar than the Argentine peso in recent months, thanks to near-daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's industrial production grew 5.8 percent year-on-year in September and 9.7 percent in October 2007 <ARECI10>. (Additional reporting by Hilary Burke; editing by Gary Crosse)\par \par \uc2 ARGENTINA-ECONOMY/INDUSTRY (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081124e4bo001kc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
