\par\par \b \uc2 Argentine business gloomy over government controls\b0\par\par\uc2 By Fiona Ortiz   \par \uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2008\par 20:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in everything from \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and private pension funds to auto factories has heightened business uncertainty even more than the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 President Cristina Fernandez and her husband and predecessor Nestor Kirchner have long used price controls to try to fight high inflation, and businesses tolerated the measures as the economy boomed from 2003 to 2007.\par \par \uc2 But with manufacturing slowing and consumer spending expected to drop, business leaders complain that the government is focused on arm-twisting companies rather than taking measures to shield the local economy.\par \par \uc2 "We can't do anything about the international situation, but on the local level the government could do things to prepare the country for what's coming, but all they do is waste time," said an executive at a local bank who asked not to be named as did all the sources who spoke to Reuters for this story.\par \par \uc2 The Kirchners are center-left leaders who have maintained a fiscal surplus and some pro-business policies, but Fernandez often makes strong speeches against unrestrained capitalism.\par \par \uc2 The labor minister has asked car manufacturers not to lay off workers as orders sink from their main market, Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the agency that oversees agricultural exports has added layers of red tape that have slowed farm shipments.\par \par \uc2 "It's absurd, it's beyond belief," said a meat exporter about shifting rules that lost him sales to China. He has scaled back buying in Argentina, one of the best-known beef producers in the world, in favor of Paraguay.\par \par \uc2 Many business leaders are also angry about the government's alleged manipulation of inflation data, but they normally complain about government policies in private because many fear time-wasting criminal investigations.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which spent $2 billion of its reserves in October to keep the peso from depreciating steeply, last week tightened controls on dollar purchases.\par \par \uc2 Banking sources said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  asked banks and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses for more information about big clients and sent official monitors to some trading floors.\par \par \uc2 The peso firmed last week to 3.3 per dollar from almost 3.4 per dollar as local media reported that Domestic Commerce Secretary Guillermo Moreno had threatened tax audits of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses that did not reverse large orders for dollars.\par \par \uc2 Reuters was not able to independently confirm the reports.\par \par \uc2 Moreno is the main enforcer of government price controls, imposed upon companies under threat of fines.\par \par \uc2 "The bad mood that seized the financial system last week and the outlook for a complete loss of transparency in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market sadly confirm the mistaken economic policy Argentina is now following," said an editorial on Monday in La Nacion, which often criticizes the Kirchners.\par \par \uc2 JUMPY\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Martin Redrado says his priority is to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because Argentines are so jumpy from past economic crises that they could make a run on banks if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciates too fast.\par \par \uc2 Fernandez's announcement last month that she would nationalize the country's $25 billion private pension fund system also unnerved the business community because it was a complete surprise and could give significant government control over companies that issue public stock and bonds.\par \par \uc2 Local bonds and stocks fell sharply after the announcement, which was interpreted as a desperate move by the government as it faces more than $20 billion in debt obligations next year.\par \par \uc2 However, the bill has met little public protest since the pension fund managers are widely considered inefficient, and it is expected to pass through Congress easily.\par \par \uc2 At the same time as it announced its takeover plans, the government launched investigations of fraud at the pension fund administrators and installed auditors at the funds.\par \par \uc2 The government is also in a tense confrontation with private Spanish company Marsans over terms of a state buyout of the country's biggest airline, \uc2 Aerolineas Argentinas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fernandez has met with executives from Spain, Argentina's biggest foreign investor, to send the message that the pension funds nationalization does not mean that Argentina is threatening private property in general.\par \par \uc2 But the message has not traveled far.\par \par \uc2 "Business people are asking themselves what is next to be grabbed by the government," said a business sector officer at one foreign embassy. (Editing by Kieran Murray)\par \par \uc2 ARGENTINA-BUSINESS/|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|G|RBN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|RNP|PGE|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081111e4bb001sn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
