\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Economic fears hit stocks, debt;  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  mixed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,074 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2008\par 19:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Emerging assets plunge on forced liquidation\par \par \uc2 * Commodities drop hit LatAm markets\par \par \uc2 * EM stocks fall sharply on the back of recession fears\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  mixed amid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds funds data)\par \par \uc2 By Manuela Badawy\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 24 (Reuters) - Emerging market assets plunged on Friday as investors unwound positions in previously profitable though risky markets on fears of a global recession.\par \par \uc2 Emerging economies are being hit by fears about the world's worst financial crisis in 80 years, falling commodity prices and the unraveling of hedge fund positions.\par \par \uc2 Government bonds of emerging economies have lost 27.75 percent of their original price so far this year, and 23.8 percent in October alone, according to \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 's Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus (EMBI+) <11EMJ><.JPMEMBIPLUS>.\par \par \uc2 Emerging sovereign debt spreads, the premium that investors demand for holding securities riskier than U.S. Treasuries, widened above 900 basis points over Treasuries for the first time in six years, before narrowing to 855 basis points later in the day.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark emerging stocks have lost 60 percent this year and are down 38 percent this month. \uc2 Morgan Stanley Capital International\uc2 's emerging markets stock index <.MSCIEF> on Friday was down 7.8 percent, while the MSCI Latin American stock index <.MILA00000PUS> fell 7.04 percent to levels not seen since August 2005.\par \par \uc2 "Emerging market valuations continued to violently adjust weaker this week, no surprise given the asset class was the star performer over the past 4-5 years and is now underperforming core markets on the downside," \uc2 RBC Dominion Securities\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "The EM de-coupling myth is clearly proving flawed with the key three pillars of emerging market support: liquidity, growth, and commodity prices -- now all simultaneously and abruptly moving in reverse mode."\par \par \uc2 Commodity prices continued their fall, hurting most of Latin American markets, which have commodity-based economies.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices <CLc1> fell as far as $62.69 a barrel, a 17-month low to close at $64.15 a barrel, as OPEC's agreement to cut 1.5 million barrels per day of supply failed to halt a price slide fueled by concerns about falling demand in a global economy headed toward recession.\par \par \uc2 Copper prices were down 24 percent for the week and have slumped over 50 percent since July's record high of $8,940 a tonne. Gold ended up snapping a three-day losing streak, as safe-haven buying emerged on fears that global stock markets were heading into a free fall. Sugar and coffee prices slid to multi-month losses and risked further falls.\par \par \uc2 The market is seeing "forced liquidation and this money flow has been hitting every commodity," said Bill O'Neill, managing partner at Logic Advisors in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 "Even if you have good assets that you would like to hold, such as some commodities perhaps, you are being forced to sell because of the liquidation pressure and margin calls, etc, along with the fact that the dollar is rallying.\par \par \uc2 "You look at these markets, whether it is agricultural or industrial commodities -- we are not trading on fundamentals, we are trading on money flow."\par \par \uc2 Investors pulled cash out of equity and debt funds in the week ended Oct. 22, with emerging market sector funds hit hardest, EPFR Global data showed on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Latin American-focused funds suffered their 20th straight week of outflows, losing a net $134.8 million to redemptions.\par \par \uc2 Europe, Middle East, and Africa funds had outflows of $189.1 million. Emerging market debt funds suffered losses for the 11th straight week. Outflows totaled $854.8 million. Long-only dedicated equity funds had outflows of $752.6 million.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS FALL, \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  MIXED\par \par \uc2 Peru's stock exchange <.IGRA> closed down nearly 7 percent weaker despite a four-hour trading halt, the latest in a series of stops called by the exchange during bouts of intense volatility.\par \par \uc2 The IGRA index fell further after trading reopened to close at 6,364.91 points. The bourse has lost some 50 percent of its value since June.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's Bovespa index <.BVSP> closed down 6.91 percent at 31,481 after touching its lowest level in five years.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's IPC index <.MXX> shed 4.6 percent to 16,978 points, its lowest since June 2006. Argentina's MerVal index <.MERV> fell more than 8 percent breaking the 900 points barrier, lowest level since June 2004. The index lost 26 percent this week and 46 percent so far this month.\par \par \uc2 Chile's blue-chip stocks <.IPSA> closed 2.22 percent lower. Chile's all-market IGPA index <.IGPA> fell 2.05 percent, while Colombia's IGBC stock index <.IGBC> lost 5.6 percent, its lowest since June 2006.\par \par \uc2 Emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed as some investors liquidated positions, dragging the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down while central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to offset a sharp fall.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso weakened 0.77 percent to 3.2775/3.2800 per dollar <ARS=RASL> in formal interbank trade, its lowest level since the January 202 devaluation. In informal trade between \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses, as tracked by Reuters, Argentina's peso lost 2.38 percent at 3.42/3.43 per U.S. dollar <ARSB=>, its lowest level since December 2002, despite a strong liquidity injection from its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real <BRBY>, closed down 0.94 percent at 2.327 per dollar even after Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold about $752 million in dollar swap contracts in a bid to counter a surge in demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso <MXN=><MEX01> rebounded after four days of steep losses to close 2.1 percent higher at 13.4175 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso <CHILJ><CLP=CL> closed down 2.8 percent to end the session at 670 per dollar, the lowest in more than five years. Its peso fell 8.7 percent against the dollar this week, bringing year-to-date losses to 25.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  placed "call" options to sell $180 million in a measure to control volatility of the peso <COP=RR> \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has weakened 13.64 percent in the last month and is down 17.19 percent against the greenback so far this year. The peso traded at 2,371 per dollar, up 1.94 percent on the day.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081027e4ao001tp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
