\par\par \b \uc2 Darling of Argentine industry, autos see downturn\b0\par\par\uc2 By Hilary Burke   \par \uc2 699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2008\par 17:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Oct 10 (Reuters) - Argentina's flourishing automotive sector is girding for a slowdown as fallout from the global credit crisis combines with high inflation and a local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is losing its competitive edge.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders are warning of possible layoffs in Latin America's third largest car-manufacturing nation, and companies such as \uc2 General Motors Corp\uc2  and \uc2 PSA Peugeot Citroen\uc2  have cut shifts this month to trim what has been extraordinary growth in production in the last six years.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the industrial lobby is upset over the recent sharp depreciation of the Brazilian real, which could make Argentine goods comparatively less competitive if -- as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said -- the Argentine peso is held firm.\par \par \uc2 Brazil is the top market for Argentine-made vehicles, so a downturn in the Brazilian economy would also spell trouble.\par \par \uc2 "We have to look very closely at the availability of credit, especially for commercial sectors ... and the impact this crisis has on Brazil's economy," said Enrique Alemany, president of Ford Argentina, an arm of \uc2 Ford Motor Co\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 "We'll see if we can turn this growth in Argentina's automotive sector into a great opportunity for the future or if it was just a phase and we'll have to shift gears at some point," Alemany told a business seminar this week.\par \par \uc2 The Ford executive said the Argentine auto industry's total production has grown fivefold from 2002 and will reach a record of 620,000 vehicles this year. Land vehicle exports account for about 9 percent of the value of Argentine exports.\par \par \uc2 Sales of locally made and imported vehicles, originally forecast at as many as 650,000 units, is now estimated at 600,000 -- which is still a record high.\par \par \uc2 Dealerships are offering discounts to lure buyers as the rosy outlook recedes.\par \par \uc2 "The automotive industry's latest figures are negative in terms of slower growth. Uncertainty is causing companies to reduce or suspend investment plans," Juan Carlos Lascurain, head of the Argentine Industrial Union, told local media.\par \par \uc2 "The very significant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation seen in our regional partner (Brazil) could imply a serious risk for some sectors," he added.\par \par \uc2 TOO MUCH INVESTMENT?\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real <BRBY> has shed 29 percent against the dollar since Aug. 1, rebounding slightly this week thanks to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has vowed to ensure demand for the peso <ARSB=> and keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from tracking the real's steep drop. The peso has weakened by nearly 9 percent since the start of August.\par \par \uc2 Asked how this could affect Argentine-based firms, Labor Minister Carlos Tomada told reporters: "The companies will analyze this, and we'll be watching how this productive process unfolds to see if employment is affected."\par \par \uc2 Tomada said, however, that the reduction in working hours at some automotive plants was due to decisions made at corporate headquarters abroad, and did not necessarily reflect a deterioration in Argentina's market conditions.\par \par \uc2 The automotive industry grew 24 percent year-on-year in the first eight months of 2008, compared with a 6.2 percent rise in overall industrial production.\par \par \uc2 But the sector's growth has slowed. August's output rose 8.0 percent from a year ago and fell 6.6 percent from July.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, high inflation -- which private estimates put at between 20 percent and 25 percent in the last year -- has sparked increasingly contentious wage negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Most recently, the SMATA labor union kicked off negotiations with car dealerships by demanding an annual raise of 50 percent -- which the dealerships said was completely out of step with current conditions.\par \par \uc2 Higher production costs are squeezing profits in the industry. But Aldo Abram of Exante consulting group said carmakers should have seen the downturn coming.\par \par \uc2 "Companies made investments in recent years that I believe were out-of-proportion ... they were clearly banking on growth in Argentine demand that won't come through, and on greater exports to Brazil, which won't happen either." (Additional reporting by Juan Bustamante; Editing by Derek Caney)\par \par \uc2 ARGENTINA-AUTOS/|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|RNP|DNP|PCO   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081010e4aa001p0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
