\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Latam central banks go it alone to curb crisis fallout\b0\par\par\uc2 By Simon Gardner   \par \uc2 815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2008\par 22:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Oct 9 (Reuters) - Far from coordinating their efforts, Latin American policy-makers will likely continue to go it alone as they seek to stabilize their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prevent the banking crisis in industrialized countries from undermining the health of emerging market economies.\par \par \uc2 Central banks in Brazil, Mexico, Chile and Colombia had been raising interest rates this year to curb inflation, but may now have to follow Europe and the United States in cutting rates as tumbling prices for energy and food undermine the value of the region's exports and threaten five years of steady economic growth.\par \par \uc2 While Latin America's government budgets and current account balances are in much better shape than they were a decade ago during the Asian and Russian crises of 1998, the policies and problems of the region's countries are different enough to prevent much coordinated regional action.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not surprised that we haven't seen concerted action. I'd be very surprised if we get it," said Rafael de la Fuente, chief economist for Latin America at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "One reason is that despite the trade ties between these countries, they're still not that integrated," he added. "Most of these are open economies, but they're open to areas other than Latin America ... on the whole."\par \par \uc2 There is no tradition of policy maker coordination in Latin America, and some say there is still likely bad blood from unilateral actions during past crises.\par \par \uc2 "Brazil's measures to try to improve competitiveness, and certainly the (depreciation) of the real, has an impact on Argentina. It's raising a lot of concerns in Argentina," said Daniel Kerner, a Latin America analyst with Eurasia Group.\par \par \uc2 "You don't have the institutional mechanisms of dialogue and revision. The problems each country faces are individual and the solutions are also for the most part individual."\par \par \uc2 Caught off guard by the scale of the worst global financial crisis since the Great Depression, some central banks ditched U.S. dollar purchases and scrambled to inject dollars into their markets. Even Mexico overcame initial hesitation and defended its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2 , LIQUIDITY FIRST\par \par \uc2 "That's the first line of defense," said Igor Arsenin, head of Latin America strategy at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  in New York. "They are going to use their quite substantial \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to try to smooth \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility."\par \par \uc2 Analysts say Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may need to consider halting a series of interest rate rises, and dig deeper into its more than $200 billion foreign reserve stockpile to help keep the real above the 3-1/2-year lows seen this week.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and dollars on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and eased mandatory reserve requirements to free up bank lending.\par \par \uc2 As fears of a deep U.S. recession mount, amid what has been dubbed as the 'perfect storm', Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also under pressure to relax monetary conditions soon for the first time since April 2006.\par \par \uc2 Analysts increasingly expect Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates in November or December, with President Alvaro Uribe keen for lower rates to foster growth which is seen slowing to around 4.0 percent this year from more than 7.0 percent in 2007.\par \par \uc2 But it is still too early to say whether Latin American central banks or governments will have to take even more drastic measures such as creating money, widening guarantees of deposits, or taking over banks, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The spreading crisis forced Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into an abrupt policy turnaround last week, calling an early halt to an $8 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  introduced earlier in the year to counter a sharp peso appreciation.\par \par \uc2 It has instead embarked on steps to inject dollars into the banking system -- \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  repurchase agreements and auctions for dollar-denominated tax revenue.\par \par \uc2 While Brazil, Mexico, Chile and Peru all enjoy investment-grade sovereign debt status, other regional economies could be harder hit by the crisis.\par \par \uc2 "(We must) rethink the role of international coordination on monetary policy and finance. It is essential to take measures, but clearly in this case we have to be careful," Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Martin Redrado told a business seminar this week.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  had already resorted to exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and price controls on goods and services after it was battered by deep economic crisis in 2001-2002 and is now in its sixth year of strong growth. But it is seen as having less flexibility in its reserves and finances. (With reporting by Elzio Barreto in Sao Paolo, Hugh Bronstein in Bogota, Noel Randewich in Mexico City, Lisa Yulkowski in Santiago and Hilary Burke in Buenos Aires)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/LATINAMERICA-CENBANKS (ANALYSI|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|G   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081009e4a9001uq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
