\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina peso hits three-month low, bonds slide\b0\par\par\uc2 403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2008\par 19:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds bonds, stocks)\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Sept 10 (Reuters) - Argentina's peso fell on Wednesday to its weakest level against the dollar in three months, while bonds slipped as investors ditched riskier emerging market assets.\par \par \uc2 The peso took a beating as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to firm against counterparts around the globe, with low foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows from exporters exacerbating the peso's slide.\par \par \uc2 It closed down 0.57 percent to 3.0725/3.0750 per dollar <ARS=RASL>, in formal trade between banks, its weakest close since June 2.\par \par \uc2 In informal trade between \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses, as measured by Reuters, the peso fell 1.52 percent to 3.1250/3.1300 per dollar <ARSB=>, its lowest level since June 17.\par \par \uc2 On world markets, the dollar <.DXY> scaled a one-year peak against the euro, its rise fueled by retreating crude oil prices and by U.S. investors cashing out of emerging markets positions and bringing their money home.\par \par \uc2 "Investors still prefer having foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions, and covering those positions is continuing to drive up the dollar," said one Argentine trader.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and traders said they expected it to step in to level the market.\par \par \uc2 On the local debt market, Argentine bonds <AR/BONOS> closed down for a second straight session, falling by 1.1 percent on average due to greater risk aversion amid fears over the health of the U.S. economy, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Among the day's biggest losers was the dollar-denominated Boden bond, maturing in 2014, which slumped 4.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 However, Argentine stocks closed with a gain of 0.61 percent, ending a run of four losses.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark MerVal <.MERV> index closed up 0.61 percent at 1,604.84 points, lifted by energy-related stocks, which rose despite falling crude prices on Wednesday due to a surprise OPEC decision to cut production.\par \par \uc2 Gainers were led by \uc2 Tenaris\uc2  , the world's top producer of seamless steel tubes for the energy industry, up 4.96 percent to 67.7 pesos per share.\par \par \uc2 Trade volume on the broad market was a meager $18 million. Of active shares, 36 retreated, 19 advanced, and 14 were unchanged. (Reporting by Walter Bianchi; Writing by Helen Popper; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ARGENTINA/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080910e49a001vb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
