\par\par \b \uc2 Latin American  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  stage steepest fall in months\b0\par\par\uc2 By Walker Simon   \par \uc2 987 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2008\par 17:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Aug 8 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  staged their sharpest fall in several months on Friday as concerns grew that the slowing global company could further weaken the prices for the region's raw materials exports.\par \par \uc2 Russia's military action in Georgia on Friday also heightened investor aversion to risky investments in emerging markets, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "The ongoing process of the U.S. dollar strengthening in the last couple weeks, combined with the news of the conflict in Georgia, has scared people, increasing risk aversion, weakening emerging markets," said Nick Chamie, head of emerging markets research in Toronto at the \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On global markets, the U.S. dollar surged Friday, on track for its biggest weekly rise in 3-1/2 years, helped by a slide in oil prices stemming partly from the slowing global economy.\par \par \uc2 As a result Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell across the region early Friday.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real <BRL=> <BRBY> tumbled 1.6 percent, its fifth straight day of losses. Mexico's peso <MXN=> <MEXO1> peso fell nearly 1.4 percent, its sharpest one-day fall in two years.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso <COP=> fell 1.9 percent, Chile's peso <CLP=> fell 1.3 percent; Peru's sol <PEN=> slid 0.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 Commodity prices continued to fall on Friday also as the U.S. dollar rose, increasing concern about economic growth in Latin America, which is a major exporter of food and fuel to world markets.\par \par \uc2 Year-to-date, Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had posted strong gains this year as central banks in the region aggressively raised interest rates to combat inflation, running at the highest annual pace in several years.\par \par \uc2 The rate rises drew in a flood of "carry trade" investors who borrow in low interest rate \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and invest the proceeds in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  offering higher yields.\par \par \uc2 Brazil, whose benchmark 13 percent interest rate is one of the world's highest after discounting for inflation, took steps to slow capital inflows as a result.\par \par \uc2 RED BALANCE OF PAYMENTS\par \par \uc2 But the lure of Brazilian interest rates is now being undercut by worries over Brazil's current account balance of payments, which recently has turned decisively into a deficit after years of hefty surpluses.\par \par \uc2 "We are nearing the point when the long Brazilian trade trade will be driven more by prospective exchange rate changes than carry considerations alone, as the risk/reward is increasingly defined by trends and a turn in the balance of payments," said RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Flavia Cattan-Nasalusky who is based in Greenwich, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's current account deficit in January through June hit $17.4 billion, the highest ever for the first half of the year since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began tracking the data in 1947.\par \par \uc2 "The broadening global slowdown carries serious risks for emerging export markets," wrote RBS' David Simmonds. "In Brazil the current account surplus has evaporated despite the positive terms of trade shock from high commodity export prices."\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Brazilian real Friday tumbled about 1.6 percent to 1.618 per dollar in Brazilian spot trade.\par \par \uc2 At the session low the real weakened about 1.8 percent day, the worst one-day drop since March 14.\par \par \uc2 COPPER POUNDS CHILE\par \par \uc2 In Chile the peso fell 1.3 percent to trade at 517.50/518.00 as prices for its main export, copper, hit a six-month low.\par \par \uc2 Peru,also a major exporter of copper and other minerals, saw its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell about 0.9 percent to 2.835 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso dropped 1.9 percent to 1,830 pesos to the dollar At session lows, the peso fell as far as 3.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Banco de Bogota\uc2  analyst David Rivera blamed the weakening emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the growing awareness that the U.S. economic slowdown was spreading around the world.\par \par \uc2 "The market had been counting on that the economies outside the United States were more resilient and data is now showing that is not the case," he said. "As this happens optimism falls in emerging markets."\par \par \uc2 MEXICAN "SUPER PESO" LOSES SHINE\par \par \uc2 In Mexico the peso <MXN=> <MEX010> fell 1.38 percent to 10.1275 per dollar, its sharpest one-day fall since July 2006.\par \par \uc2 The peso hit a six-year high on Monday at 9.8745 pesos, bringing back memories of the country's "super peso," as it was dubbed by market players and the financial press.\par \par \uc2 The landmark moment was Friday, Aug. 1, when the dollar weakened to below 10 pesos for the first time since 2002.\par \par \uc2 "There was a move to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appetite for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  following its recent statements about not favoring a strong peso," said RBC's Chamie.\par \par \uc2 A key resistance level was at 9.85 pesos to the dollar. When the peso approached that level concern about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  grew and some investors covered short positions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso also fell, taking a beating from jitters over the state of the country's economy and investor financing concerns, as well as the dollar rally.\par \par \uc2 "Political issues are influencing the country's economic variables and investor mood at a time when all emerging markets are falling," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader in Buenos Aires.\par \par \uc2 In formal interbank trade, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the peso was trading down 0.16 percent at 3.05/3.0525 per dollar <ARS=RASL>. In informal trade between \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses, as measured by Reuters, the peso was down 0.24 to end at 3.07/3.0725 per dollar <ARSB=>. (Additional reporting by Helen Popper in Buenos Aires, Nelson Bocanegra in Santiago, Lisa Yulkowski in Santiago, Michael O'Boyle in Mexico City, Ricardo Serra in Lima; Elzio Barreto and Silvio Cascione in Sao Paulo).\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /LATAM|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080811e488001tw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
