\par\par \b \uc2 Argentine stocks slip in line with regional losses\b0\par\par\uc2 437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2008\par 23:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, July 1 (Reuters) - Argentine bonds and stocks fell on Tuesday in tandem with regional markets hit by fears over the impact of inflation and soaring oil prices, while the peso firmed amid \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  The benchmark MerVal stock index <.MERV> slipped 0.6 percent to end at 2,095.12 points, after dipping by as much as 2.22 percent during the day -- after two sessions of gains led by energy-related stocks.\par \par \uc2  Banking issues picked up at the end of the day after financial shares on Wall Street <.DJI> recovered as investors looked for bargains.\par \par \uc2  "The MerVal followed Brazil <.BVSP> and Mexico <.MXX>, pulled by \uc2 Tenaris\uc2  due to its weighting," said Horacio Corneille, who runs a brokerage that bears his name. "The rise of the banks was logical when you looked at the gains of sector in the United States."\par \par \uc2  Banco Hipotecario  jumped 5.31 percent while Grupo Financiero Galicia  rose 3.18 percent.\par \par \uc2  The biggest loser was \uc2 Tenaris\uc2   , the world's biggest maker of seamless steel pipes for the energy industry, down 2.34 percent on profit-taking to 116.8 pesos. \uc2 Tenaris\uc2  had driven the MerVal's gains in the prior two sessions.\par \par \uc2  Trade volume remained moderate at $32.5 million. Of active issues, 33 rose, 84 fell and 14 were unchanged.\par \par \uc2  BONDS, PESO\par \par \uc2  On the local debt market, Argentine bonds <AR/BONOS> slipped 0.9 percent on average in over-the-counter trade in a market underpinned by jitters over the U.S. economy and greater risk aversion among investors, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's weakest performers included the dollar-denominated Discount bond, which finished down 2.3 percent.\par \par \uc2  On the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the peso <ARS=RASL> firmed to 3.02/3.0225 per dollar, its strongest level for 31 months due to dollar sales by grains exporters and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.\par \par \uc2  In informal trade between \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses, as measured by Reuters, the peso appreciated by 0.49 percent to close at 3.0425/3.045 per dollar <ARSB=>.\par \par \uc2  Traders said the continued \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  meant \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso was often moving against the trend of other regional money markets, where local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell against the greenback on Tuesday due to the oil-price rally.\par \par \uc2  The bank has been selling dollars to bolster the peso amid uncertainty surrounding a farm dispute that began in mid-March over export taxes on soy.  (Reporting by Jorge Otaola and Walter Bianchi; Writing by Helen Popper; Editing by Leslie Adler)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ARGENTINA/|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080701e471001np\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
