\par\par \b \uc2 With New Bank Rule, Argentina Ctrl Bk Adds More Fuel To Peso\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Casey   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,107 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2008\par 21:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Having spent billions of dollars to defy wary investors and drive the Argentine peso to a two-and-a-half-year high, the Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week briefly gave it more fuel with a surprise change to bank accounting rules.\par \par \uc2 When the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  told local banks last week that they would have to count their clients' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings in their own "global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  position" - making them subject to the same limits - an unwinding of dollar positions ensued.\par \par \uc2 Accordingly, the peso strengthened by two centavos to close at a new two-and-a-half-year high of ARS3.0175 to the dollar Tuesday, a level it again held Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Thursday saw a sudden reversal, with the peso closing at ARS3.0275 to the dollar. But this was still a far cry from the ARS3.19 lows of one month ago, when the peso was going through its weakest moment since 2003 as a bitter farm crisis stirred fears of financial turmoil.\par \par \uc2 Hopes now exist that Congress will find a face-saving solution to the intense dispute over a grains export tax, ending three months of protests and blockades by farmers and truck owners. But there is no guarantee that the crisis will be resolved. Meanwhile, economic reports, such as one this week showing a 0.3% on-month decline in May industrial production, clearly points to economic damage.\par \par \uc2 With Argentines still nervously drawing down their peso deposits - and shifting into dollars at the attractive rates the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is delivering - most traders and analysts are convinced the exchange rate has become a weapon in the government's battle with the farmers.\par \par \uc2 "The gain cannot be seen in terms of economics; it's a political phenomenon," said Noelia Lucini, an analyst at Capital Markets \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  disputes such claims, saying its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is about preventing volatility, and claiming it doesn't target exchange rates. At a conference last Thursday, bank president Martin Redrado defended the expenditure of almost $3 billion in reserves in two months, arguing that it would have been "absurd" not to tap its $50 billion war chest to defend the peso during this crisis.\par \par \uc2 But why overshoot by so much? A rally of the magnitude seen this past month had not occurred since the end of the 2002 financial crisis, largely because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's diligent, daily dollar purchases had stopped the peso from following other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' gains versus the greenback.\par \par \uc2 So regardless of what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says, the market is reading what seems like blatant exchange rate signals. The talk now is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  is targeting ARS3.00. And the change in bank accounting rules seems to confirm that.\par \par \uc2 Yet it's not clear how long this can last without Redrado's institution spending more money or without hurting the financial system. Indeed, the accounting rule also prompted a decline in money market rates and, while this was no doubt also desired by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  worried about stress to the financial system, it could also make the peso inherently less attractive to investors and savers. Thursday's one-centavo reversal of the prior days' gains could prove indicative.\par \par \uc2 As the dollar positions were unwound on Monday and Tuesday and as money flooded into the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's three-month Nobac certificates - a benchmark peso-based security - their nominal rate fell by more than 350 basis points this week, according to a research report by \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analysts.\par \par \uc2 Before the regulatory change, relatively low implied rates on peso-dollar hedges in the non-deliverable forward, or NDF, market had made the three-month Nobac's 17.6% nominal yield an attractive play, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 But after it dropped to 13.48% while the accompanying price jump delivered a tidy capital gain to anyone who had taken advantage of the spread, it was time to get out, the bank's analysts concluded.\par \par \uc2 "(We) believe the interest rate move in our direction is likely to be short-lived, and prefer to take profits," they wrote.\par \par \uc2 Initially at least, the local banks' unwinding of both their spot dollar positions and their offsetting NDF hedges helps the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which was exposed in the forwards market due to the locally traded futures contracts it had used in lieu of even more spot market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But as \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analyst Eduardo Levy-Yeyati pointed out, the new phase of interest rate equilibrium - the narrowing in the spread between the implied NDF and the nominal Nobac rates - could give way to new selling pressure on peso securities.\par \par \uc2 Lower nominal rates means greater peso liquidity and to the extent that those fresh pesos go chasing dollars, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will again have to use reserves to prop up the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Such flight remains a distinct possibility when concerns about political and economic stability remain. Many observers expect voter-wary lawmakers to break their allegiance to President Cristina Fernandez and amend her contested tax bill - a move that should prevent a return of farmer protests. But the process is not going smoothly.\par \par \uc2 There have been altercations in the plaza outside the Congress, where pro- and anti-government protesters have set up competing tents. Inside the building, pro-government lawmakers in the ruling majority have frustrated opposition counterparts with stalling tactics.\par \par \uc2 Just last week, a survey by Poliarquia put Fernandez's popularity rating at a mere 20%, while Torcuato di Tella University said its consumer confidence index had dropped to its lowest level since March 2003.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the farm crisis is only a small part of what's unnerving Argentine consumers and bank clients. Of bigger concern, arguably, is the threat of inflation.\par \par \uc2 With annual price gains thought to be running as high as 25% - and with surveys showing that virtually no one believes the official consumer price index reading of 9.1% - economists warn that any measures making interest rates even more deeply negative in real terms will further dissuade Argentines from keeping their savings in pesos.\par \par \uc2 Although he believes the new rules will lead banks to stop offering dollar deposits as an alternative to such peso accounts, \uc2 Macrovision\uc2  economist Esteban Medrano warns that savers won't simply go back to peso deposits. Instead they'll put their money in an investment option that Argentines often use at such moments, one of little benefit to the financial system: safety deposit boxes.\par \par \uc2 -By Michael Casey, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4590 2428; michael.j.casey@dowjones.com [ 26-06-08 1912GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080626e46q000vz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
