\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Firms As  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Changes Bets; Bonds Firm\b0\par\par\uc2 By Drew Benson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2008\par 23:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--The Argentine peso firmed more than a centavo against the dollar Friday, despite an entrenched farm conflict, as days of heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar selling appeared to push \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators to change their bets in favor of an even-stronger peso.\par \par \uc2 The peso closed at ARS3.10 against the dollar in interbank trading, from Thursday's close at ARS3.1125. That marked peso's strongest point since June of last year, and a sharp turnaround from the five-year weak point of ARS3.1825 it hit amid farm-crisis concerns on April 22. To push the peso, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had to sell some $1.4 billion in reserves, a move traders say is more political than logical monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 "It's a show of strength, a response to the farmers" who have been protesting a grain export tax hike since March, said Hector Blanco, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with local brokerage ABC Mercado de Cambio. For nearly five years, the government has regularly bought dollars to maintain a weak peso that favors exporters. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders think this deliberate strengthening of the peso by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a way to show cereal exporters just who is in charge.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was apparently not buying peso bonds Friday as it had in previous sessions in a bid to shore up local debt prices, said local fixed-income analyst Noelia Lucini of Capital Markets Argentina.\par \par \uc2 Still, demand was strong for peso notes, with the price of the Discount rising 2.8% to ARS102.70, with a yield of 10.34%, while the Par in pesos ended 2% higher at ARS33.15, with a yield of 9.65%.\par \par \uc2 In a report Friday, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said it expects that the Argentina government's offer Thursday night to rework grain export taxes will weaken and splinter farm protests, despite initial farm leader rejection of the new measures.\par \par \uc2 That's because subsidies for small and medium farmers will make them less likely to protest as time goes on, while the planting season represents a time constraint.\par \par \uc2 "We find these arguments convincing enough to believe that, after the announcement of the government's proposal, the farmers' conflict will gradually dilute into non-disruptive demonstrations and media rhetoric," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  wrote.\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  recommended dollar notes, the Discount in dollars and Boden 2012s. The local Discount in dollars ended 0.75% lower at ARS263, while the Boden 2012 rose 0.36% to ARS282.\par \par \uc2 On the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange, the benchmark Merval Index slid 0.81% on farm-crisis uncertainty to 2,205.72 points. Among losers, Banco Frances BBVA (BFR) slid 5.73% to ARS6.41 in heavy trading, while Grupo Financiero Galicia (GGAL.BA) ended 4.81% lower at ARS1.78.\par \par \uc2 -By Drew Benson, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; andrew.benson@dowjones.com [ 30-05-08 2131GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080530e45u00133\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
