\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Markets Slide As Farm Talks Stall;Peso At 5-Yr Low\b0\par\par\uc2 By Drew Benson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 780 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2008\par 23:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentine stocks and bonds slid Tuesday on a spike in investor uncertainty fueled by news that key farm talks were suspended, a further sign that disruptive farmer roadblocks will resume next month.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the peso reached its lowest point since March 2003 to close at ARS3.1825 against the dollar in interbank trading, slightly weaker than Monday's close at ARS3.18. The peso had weakened as far as ARS3.1875 by mid-afternoon, but recovered some ground amid apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In New York, \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  was by far the worst performer on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's Emerging Markets Bond Index Global Diversified, dropping 25 basis points to 585 basis points, for a negative 2.56% return. The broader EMBIGD was 2 basis points wider with a 0.07% negative return. Argentina's 2033 discount bonds late Tuesday were down 1 13/16 at 74 7/16 bid, while the 2038 par bonds were still off 1/2 at 36 even, according to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "There's been negative sentiment toward Argentina for a week or so now," said a New York-based emerging markets debt trader, adding that the latest headlines aren't helping. "People need something positive that they can sink their teeth into, and they're not getting it," he added. The credit has also been hurt by recent news that a U.S. federal judge has frozen Argentine bonds with a face value of up to $17 billion ahead of a hearing next week involving investor "holdouts" who rejected the harsh terms of the nation's 2005 debt restructuring.\par \par \uc2 In the local Argentine debt market, concern this week has come from the sense that "the farm conflict talks are closer to failing than reaching an agreement," said fixed-income analyst Paulino Seoane, also of Lopez Leon Brokers. "That adds to worry that there isn't any real or sustainable measure to contain inflation," which undermines debt value.\par \par \uc2 Farm group leaders Tuesday stepped up threats to resume roadblocks after a 30-day truce launched on April 2 expires, while news emerged by late afternoon that the day's key wheat talks between farm group leaders and government officials had been suspended.\par \par \uc2 A three-week farm strike last month led to food shortages in the Argentine capital, and helped accelerate inflation that economists already saw running at 20% on the year. Farmers launched the strike in response to a government increase in soybean export taxes.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Discount in pesos neared its record low of ARS100, seen during last summer's subprime crisis. "But yields are at a record now, higher than last year, due to inflation index adjustments," Seoane said. The Discount closed at ARS101, with a yield of 10.35%, while the Bogar 2018, another peso note linked to inflation, fell to ARS134.65, with a yield of 11.45%.\par \par \uc2 Investors also are concerned about the sliding peso, Seoane added. "It's still not clear if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to hold the peso or let it weaken further," he said.\par \par \uc2 Daniel Bou Kahir, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at local Banco de la Pampa, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently sold about $100 million during the final 15 to 20 minutes of trading in a bid to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bou Kahir said this week's rise in political uncertainty has boosted demand for dollars from institutional investors and banks, as well as at the exchange house level as individuals seek more dollars and euros.\par \par \uc2 He said the heavy late-session \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could put the breaks on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide, although it is clear that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is comfortable with a weaker peso. "There is some speculation that it could go to ARS3.20," Bou Kahir said.\par \par \uc2 On the Buenos Aires Stock Exchange, the benchmark Merval Index, which had been resisting local political noise in recent sessions amid a mild upswing, dropped 2.48% to 2,147.00. Volume was a very heavy ARS342.9 million ($107.7 million).\par \par \uc2 Among the losers, \uc2 Tenaris\uc2  (TS) ended 1.67% lower at ARS88, while Grupo Financiero Galicia (GGAL) slid 5.96% to ARS2.05.\par \par \uc2 "It's not just the farm sector" and a sliding peso that has shaken investors, said stock analyst Juan Jose Vazquez of local Bull Market Brokers. He also attributed rising uncertainty to some newspaper rumors of a Cabinet shakeup that could replace Cabinet Chief Alberto Fernandez and Economy Minister Martin Lousteau.\par \par \uc2 -By Drew Benson, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4311-3127; andrew.benson@dowjones.com [ 22-04-08 2100GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080422e44m0013b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
