\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Argentine peso still bucks regional trend, for now   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hilary Burke   \par \uc2 809 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 22:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, March 4 (Reuters) - The Argentine peso has become Latin America's great anomaly, holding steady against the U.S. dollar as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of neighboring countries have risen in the past year.   \par \par \uc2 The government's persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the Argentine peso and buy U.S. dollars has managed to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak, an effort that may have been made easier by high inflation surpassed only by Venezuela in the region.   \par \par \uc2 The weak peso has helped the government foster an export boom, and take advantage of soaring international commodity prices, to stimulate economic growth and refill its coffers after a debt default and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation in 2002.   \par \par \uc2 Growing global inflation pressures may force the Argentine government into letting the peso appreciate to help contain rising consumer prices, but such a change in monetary policy would undermine government's export-led growth strategy -- and some analysts predict instead further nominal depreciation.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Brazil, Colombia, Peru and Chile have hit multiple-year highs against the U.S. dollar in recent months, as the greenback sinks against free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  worldwide.   \par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like China and other protectionist countries, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep its exports competitive. The Argentine peso <ARSB=> slipped 2.7 percent last year and has traded stable against the U.S. dollar since late December.   \par \par \uc2 "It could be that the peso should appreciate, that's the theory held by scholars and technocrats," said Fernando Izzo, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  brokerage ABC Mercado de Cambios.   \par \par \uc2 "But the theory doesn't hold in Argentina, where the exchange rate is controlled and moved up and down by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2 President Cristina Fernandez, who succeeded her husband in office three months ago, touts the role of a weak peso in stimulating "re-industrialization" after a one-to-one peg to the U.S. dollar shut down factories in the 1990s.   \par \par \uc2 The government has held the peso steady at roughly 3.0 per dollar for nearly four-and-a-half years, mainly by having the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buy dollars and then issue debt to neutralize the inflationary impact of expanding the monetary supply.   \par \par \uc2 As a result, the country's foreign reserves have surged to some $49.3 billion from $10.3 billion five years ago.   \par \par \uc2 Rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are not unique to Argentina. Many emerging market governments are stockpiling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as commodities prices boom.   \par \par \uc2 The hitch is that surging grain and crude oil costs are also stoking inflation globally. And one way for governments to ease price pressures linked to imports is by letting their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate.   \par \par \uc2 EXPORT FIESTA   \par \par \uc2 Inflation is a stubborn problem in Argentina, where the official annual rate of around 8.0 percent is widely discredited and private estimates go as high as 25 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Fernandez, like her husband, rejects anti-inflation tactics such as monetary tightening and backs instead unorthodox measures like price caps and export bans.   \par \par \uc2 "The (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) policy is \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protectionist, favoring industry and jobs," former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Rodolfo Rossi said. "If the peso were allowed to float freely, this would have a huge impact on the workforce."   \par \par \uc2 Investment bank  \uc2 Bear Stearns\uc2  said that strong export-related inflows could overpower Argentina's ability to keep the peso weak without worsening inflation.   \par \par \uc2 "Unless grain prices fall materially as the year evolves or the public sector decides to save much more money ... authorities will likely be forced to accept at least some nominal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation this year -- perhaps all the way towards the 2.9 per dollar level," the bank said last month.   \par \par \uc2 Some other analysts disagree, noting that industry groups are lobbying for an even weaker peso as inflation and hefty salary rises erode their competitive edge.   \par \par \uc2 They also say the export boom benefits the government, too, thanks to heavy taxes on soy, grain and fuel sales abroad.   \par \par \uc2 An analyst at Hallgarten & Company consultancy called Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime unsustainable and said a large hedge fund investor like George Soros could try to force the peso to strengthen.   \par \par \uc2 But Izzo said capital controls and other regulations would make such a move difficult, and Rossi agreed this was not likely but said Argentina should stay vigilant to prevent it.   \par \par \uc2 One point in Argentina's favor is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been making fewer dollar purchases in recent months, and has issued less debt, as the Treasury taps its fiscal surplus to help sustain the peso.   \par \par \uc2 "That's more genuine because it doesn't imply money injection," said Pablo Morra, an economist at  \uc2 Goldman Sachs.\uc2  "The government's policy approach is facing some challenges, but in the very near future I think it can still succeed, particularly if the Treasury helps."   \par \par \uc2 ARGENTINA-PESO (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080307e434001x9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
