\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina stocks drop ahead of U.S. Fed meeting   \b0\par\par\uc2 395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2007\par 23:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Argentine stocks lost ground on Monday along with global markets as investors awaited the outcome of Tuesday's U.S. Federal Reserve meeting, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 The MerVal index <.MERV> of 21 leading stocks fell 0.86 percent to 2,031.25 points, while on Wall Street the Dow Jones industrial average <.DJI> lost 0.29 percent.   \par \par \uc2 "Investors continued with certain doubts about what the Fed will decide tomorrow," said Hernan Labrone, an analyst with Fenix Financial Company.   \par \par \uc2 Most analysts and investors expect the Fed to cut the federal funds rate by a quarter of a percentage point, which would cushion the impact of recent financial turbulence on the U.S. economy.   \par \par \uc2 Telecommunications company  \uc2 Telecom Argentina\uc2  fell 3.29 percent to 14.7 pesos per share in Buenos Aires, while real estate firm IRSA followed close behind, slipping 3.06 percent to 4.75 pesos per share.   \par \par \uc2 Volume on the broad market was a weak 40.5 million pesos ($12.7 million). Of active shares, 21 advanced, 40 declined and 11 were unchanged.   \par \par \uc2 BONDS, PESO   \par \par \uc2 On the debt market, Argentine bonds <AR/BONOS> finished steady as cautious investors awaited the Fed's decision.   \par \par \uc2 Losses were led by dollar-denominated Discount paper, which dropped 0.6 percent on average across several markets, while the Bonar 2017 in dollars led gains with a 0.9 percent increase.   \par \par \uc2 The peso maintained its positive trend against the dollar, with exporters cashing in their dollar earnings for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 In informal trade between \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses, as measured by Reuters, the Argentine peso <ARSB=> firmed 0.16 percent to end at 3.1900/3.1925 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 It closed stable at 3.1350/3.1375 pesos per dollar in formal interbank trade <ARS=RASL>, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on an almost daily basis.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected 10 million pesos into money markets on Monday through the auction of variable-rate repurchase agreements, part of its strategy to keep down short-term interbank lending rates by increasing liquidity.   \par \par \uc2 Independently, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offered up to 1 billion pesos ($313 million) of seven-day, fixed-rate repos in a direct sale to banks to offset the jump in short-term rates.   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ARGENTINA/|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070917e39h001yo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
