\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina stocks, bonds rise amid regional upswing   \b0\par\par\uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2007\par 23:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Aug 24 (Reuters) - Argentine stocks closed higher on Friday for the fifth straight session, mirroring gains in bourses throughout the region following the release of surprisingly strong U.S. economic data, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 At the same time, bonds ended higher on average and the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed slightly after hitting its weakest point since January 2003 in the previous session.   \par \par \uc2 The benchmark MerVal <.MERV> stock index closed 2.11 percent higher at 2,081.45 points, although the index is still down 4.5 percent since Aug. 1 due to global market woes.   \par \par \uc2 Brazilian and Mexican stocks also closed higher, and the Dow Jones industrial average <.DJI> rose 1.08 percent due to robust U.S. data on home sales and factory orders.   \par \par \uc2 "There is no doubt that with the Dow Jones growing by 1 percent, those investors who are still liquid are willing to buy stocks," said Leopoldo Olivari, a trader at Bacque brokerage.   \par \par \uc2 The MerVal's biggest gainer was aluminum maker Aluar , whose shares surged 6.75 percent to 6.17 pesos.   \par \par \uc2 Trade volume on the broader market was a hefty 125.2 million pesos ($39 million). Among active shares, 60 rose, 8 fell and 13 were unchanged.   \par \par \uc2 Argentine bonds ended 1.1 percent higher on average <AR/BONOS>, with some paper rising as much as 1.5 percent and other bonds dipping slightly in what traders described as thin trade volume.   \par \par \uc2 In trade between \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses, as measured by Reuters, the peso <ARSB=> closed just 0.08 percent firmer at 3.2250/3.2275 per U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 In formal interbank trade, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso closed 0.24 stronger at 3.1700/3.1725 <ARS=RASL>, with traders saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold around $100 million in dollars.   \par \par \uc2 Before the latest market turbulence -- spurred by worries over tight credit in the U.S. subprime mortgage market -- the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  normally bought dollars to keep the peso from appreciating and keep exports competitive.   \par \par \uc2 But since the peso began depreciating, it has resorted mainly to selling dollars. (Additional reporting by Nelson Lampert)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ARGENTINA/|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070824e38o001sw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
