\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina peso falls to 4-1/2-yr low, stocks rise   \b0\par\par\uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2007\par 22:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Aug 23 (Reuters) - Argentina's peso ended at its weakest point since January 2003 on Thursday as investors kept buying dollars amid fears of more market volatility and after taking cues from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, stocks rose and bonds ended steady.   \par \par \uc2 In thin trade between \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses, as measured by Reuters, the peso <ARSB=> closed down 0.7 percent at 3.2275/3.2300 per dollar. This was weaker than last Thursday, when global financial markets dived on worries about the impact of tight credit in the U.S. subprime mortgage market.   \par \par \uc2 In formal interbank trade, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso closed 0.32 percent weaker at 3.1775/3.1800 <ARS=RASL>.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said interbank trading was more active, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying and selling dollars, which was seen as tacit support for the peso's current price.   \par \par \uc2 "The dollar strengthened again (against the peso) because banks and private parties continued their purchases due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's steady rise and also because they interpreted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as backing this trend," said Fernando Izzo, a trader at ABC Mercado de Cambios.   \par \par \uc2 The peso has slumped 1.8 percent since Aug. 1 in both the informal and interbank markets.   \par \par \uc2 Before the latest market turbulence, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  normally bought dollars to keep the peso from appreciating and keep exports competitive. But since the peso began depreciating, it has resorted mainly to selling dollars.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves hover near record highs at some $43.7 billion <BCRA32>.   \par \par \uc2 The benchmark MerVal <.MERV> stock index closed 1.48 percent higher at 2,038.29 points. But the index is still down around 6.5 percent since Aug. 1 due to global market woes, which pushed Argentine stocks to a 10-month low last week.   \par \par \uc2 The MerVal's biggest gainer was  \uc2 Telecom Argentina\uc2  , which jumped 9 percent to end at 16.15 pesos a share. Traders said this company had been hard-hit during recent losses and was recovering lost ground.   \par \par \uc2 Trade volume on the broader market was a robust 112.4 million pesos ($34.8 million). Among active shares, 63 rose, 26 fell and 14 were unchanged.   \par \par \uc2 Argentine bonds ended just 0.2 percent lower on average <AR/BONOS>, after a strong rebound on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Investors' jitters have calmed somewhat due to increasing expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve could lower interest rates next month to calm market volatility, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 When interest rates fall in the United States, riskier but higher-yielding emerging markets assets tend to be viewed as more attractive. (Additional reporting by Nelson Lampert)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ARGENTINA|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070823e38n00208\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
