\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina bonds, stocks buoyed by Fed statements   \b0\par\par\uc2 319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2007\par 18:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Aug 8 (Reuters) - Argentine bonds gained 2 percent on average and stocks were also up around 2 percent in midday trading on Wednesday, as emerging markets reacted favorably to the U.S. Federal Reserve's latest statements.   \par \par \uc2 The biggest mover among Argentine bonds, which were hard hit by recent global market losses, was the discount bond denominated in dollars, which surged 4 percent in over-the-counter trade.   \par \par \uc2 The Fed said on Tuesday that a housing slump should not stall U.S. economic growth, reducing market jitters about the overall health of the American economy and favoring riskier emerging markets assets along with Wall Street stocks.   \par \par \uc2 Argentine debt spreads over comparable U.S. Treasuries narrowed by a full 54 basis points to 405 basis points on  \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 's EMBI+ bond index <11EMJ>.   \par \par \uc2 "Institutional investors are once again taking positions in bonds and the market is tentatively becoming attractive again," a trader said. "We are seeing investors shift positions from the (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) market to bonds."   \par \par \uc2 The benchmark MerVal stock index <.MERV> was up nearly 2 percent at 2,162.75 points, with trade volume on the broad market at around 48 million pesos ($15 million).   \par \par \uc2 Telecom Argentina\uc2  was leading the session's gains with a 5.6 percent rise to 14.10 pesos a share, a day before the company is due to report quarterly earnings.   \par \par \uc2 On the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the peso firmed slightly against the U.S. dollar in midday trade.   \par \par \uc2 In informal trade between \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses, as measured by Reuters, the peso was 0.08 percent stronger at 3.1800/3.1825 pesos per dollar <ARSB=>. And it was trading 0.24 percent firmer at 3.1325/3.1350 in formal interbank trade <ARS=RASL>, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 ARGENTINA\b0 \highlight\uc2 /|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070808e388001oc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
