\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina stocks slide on local inflation data   \b0\par\par\uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2007\par 00:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Reuters) - Argentine stocks and bonds fell Monday, reacting to news of lower-than-expected domestic inflation data that hit bank paper especially hard, as the returns on their bonds are adjusted for inflation.   \par \par \uc2 The benchmark MerVal index fell 1.89 percent to end at 2,107.81 after sinking as much as 2.26 percent during the session. Trade volume on the broad market was moderate at $14.4 million, and among active issues, 10 advanced, 61 declined and 6 were unchanged.   \par \par \uc2 The government announced the consumer price index rose 0.5 percent in July. But analysts polled by Reuters said they were expecting a rise of at least 0.7 percent.   \par \par \uc2 "The MerVal loss delivered a message directly to the government following the announcement of the low inflation data," said Augusto Farina, a trader with Amirante-Galitis brokerage.   \par \par \uc2 "We are continuing to lose opportunities," he added, noting that most other regional and emerging markets closed with gains.   \par \par \uc2 Stock indexes throughout the rest of Latin America fell in the morning but bounced back late in the day, responding to an afternoon rally in U.S. stocks.   \par \par \uc2 Shares in Grupo Financiero Galicia were especially hard hit, falling 4.58 percent to 2.71 pesos per share, as was Banco Hiptecario, which slumped 3.02 percent to 2.57 pesos per share.   \par \par \uc2 Argentine bonds fell an average 1.3 percent in local trade, with the benchmark Disc peso-denominated bond dropping 1.6 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Emerging market debt has been hit hard by the U.S. lending crisis, which has led investors to avoid assets in risky markets.   \par \par \uc2 "The market was hoping that July's inflation data would be better and therefore the bond market, once adjusted for inflation, didn't cut its losses like the rest of the region," said one trader.   \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market the peso closed little changed against the U.S. dollar. In informal trade between \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses, as measured by Reuters, the peso closed flat at 3.1825/3.1850 pesos per dollar, while strengthening 0.08 percent to 3.1375/3.1400 in formal interbank trade, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 ARGENTINA\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070806e3860024a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
